window.BAHR_DATA=window.BAHR_DATA||{};window.BAHR_DATA["passages-03"]=[{"id":"q2-45-46","q":"2:45–46","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:45–46 · Patience, prayer and certainty of meeting God","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 101–103","cairo":"I:101–103","source_page_first":101,"source_page_last":103,"ar":"وَاسْتَعِينُوا بِالصَّبْرِ وَالصَّلاةِ وَإِنَّها لَكَبِيرَةٌ إِلاَّ عَلَى الْخاشِعِينَ (45) الَّذِينَ يَظُنُّونَ أَنَّهُمْ مُلاقُوا رَبِّهِمْ وَأَنَّهُمْ إِلَيْهِ راجِعُونَ (46)\nقلت: الصبر: هو حبس القلب على حكم الرب، فيحتمل أن يُراد به ظاهره، أو يراد به هنا الصوم، لأن فيه الصبر عن الشهوات. والخشوع فى الجوارح: سكونها وذُلها، والخضوع في القلب: انقياده لحكم الرب.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: يا مَن ابتلي بالرئاسة والجاه، واستكبر عن الانقياد لأحكام الله التي جاءت بها الرسل من عند الله، استعن على نفسك بِالصَّبْرِ على قطع المألوفات، وترك الحظوظ والشهوات، وأصل فروعها حب الرئاسة والجاه، فمن صبر على تركهما فاز برضوان الله. وفي الحديث: «وفي الصبر على ما تكره خير كثير» . وقال الشاعر:\nوالصَّبْرُ كالصْبرِ مُرٌ في مذَاقَتِه ... لَكِنْ عَواقِبُه أحلَى مِن العسلِ\nأو: وَاسْتَعِينُوا بالصوم وَالصَّلاةِ، فإن في الصوم كَسْرَ الشَّهْوَةِ وتصفية النفس، فإذا صفت النفس من الرذائل تحلت بأنواع الفضائل، كالتواضع والإنصاف، والخشوع وسائر سني الأوصاف، وفي الصلاة أنواع من العبادات النفسية والبدنية، كالطهارة، وستر العورة، وصرف المال فيهما، والتوجه إلى الكعبة، والعكوف للعبادة، وإظهار الخشوع بالجوارح، وإخلاص النية بالقلب، ومجاهدة الشيطان، ومناجاة الرحمن وقراءة القرآن، وكف النفس عن الأطْيَبَيْنِ(1)، وفي الصلاة قضاء المآرب وجبر المصائب، ولذلك كان- عليه الصلاة والسلام- إذا حزبه أمر فزع إلى الصلاة، وَإِنَّها لَكَبِيرَةٌ أي: شاقة على النفس لتكريرها في كل يوم، ومجيئها وقت حلاوة النوم، إِلَّا عَلَى الْخاشِعِينَ الذين سكنت حلاوتها في قلوبهم، وتناجوا فيها مع ربهم، حتى صارت فيها قُرَّة عينهم.\nالذين يتيقنون أَنَّهُمْ مُلاقُوا رَبِّهِمْ فيتنعمون بالنظر إلى وجهه الكريم، ويتيقنون أيضاً أنهم راجعون إلى ربهم بالبعث والحشر للثواب والعقاب، وإنما عبَّر الحق تعالى هنا بالظن في موضع اليقين إبقاء على المذنبين، وتوفراً على العاصين، الذين ليس لهم صفاء اليقين إذ لو ذكر اليقين صرفاً لخرجوا من الجملة، فسبحانه من رب حليم، وجواد كريم. اللهم امنن علينا بصفاء المعرفة واليقين، حتى لا يختلج قلوبنا وَهْمٌ ولا ريب، يا رب العالمين.\nالإشارة: يا من رام الدخول إلى حضرة الله، تذلل وتواضع لأولياء الله، وتجرّع الصبر في ذلك كي يدخلوك حضرة الله، كما قال القائل:\nتَذللْ لِمنْ تَهْوَى فَلَيْسَ الْهَوى سَهْلُ(2) ... إِذَا رَضِيَ المحبوبُ صَحّ لَكَ الوَصْلُ\nفإن منعك من ذلك حب الرئاسة والجاه، فاستعن على ذلك بالصبر والصلاة، فإن الصبر عنوان الظفر، والصلاة تنهى عن الفحشاء والمنكر. فأدمن قرع الباب حتى تدخل مع الأحباب، فالإدمان على عبادة الصلاة أمره كبير، إلا مَن خلص إلى مناجاة العلي الكبير، وتحقق بملاقاة الشهود والعيان، ورجع إلى مولاه في كل أوان، فإن الصلاة حينئذٍ تكون له من قرّة العين. وبالله التوفيق، وهو الهادي إلى سواء الطريق.\nولما أمرهم بالأصول والفروع، ذكَّرهم بالنعم، وخوفهم بالوعيد على عدم شكرها، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَاسْتَعِينُوا بِالصَّبْرِ وَالصَّلاةِ وَإِنَّها لَكَبِيرَةٌ إِلاَّ عَلَى الْخاشِعِينَ (45) الَّذِينَ يَظُنُّونَ أَنَّهُمْ مُلاقُوا رَبِّهِمْ وَأَنَّهُمْ إِلَيْهِ راجِعُونَ (46)","quran_en":"45. Seek help through patience and prayer. It is indeed burdensome, except for the humble: 46. those who anticipate meeting their Lord and returning to Him.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Patience, fasting and humility","html":"<p>I say: patience (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الصبر<\/bdi>) is holding the heart fast to the Lord’s judgement. The word may retain its outward meaning, or here denote fasting, since fasting involves patience in abstaining from desires. Humility of the limbs (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الخشوع<\/bdi>) is their stillness and abasement; submission in the heart (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الخضوع<\/bdi>) is its yielding to the Lord’s judgement.<\/p><p class=\"translator-note-ref\">[Translator’s print-source notes: <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/R07-03\">[R07-03]<\/a>]<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: patience ( الصبر ) is holding the heart fast to the Lord’s judgement. The word may retain its outward meaning, or here denote fasting, since fasting involves patience in abstaining from desires. Humility of the limbs ( الخشوع ) is their stillness and abasement; submission in the heart ( الخضوع ) is its yielding to the Lord’s judgement. [Translator’s print-source notes: [R07-03] ]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The disciplines that overcome love of rank","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: you who are afflicted by leadership and rank, and are too proud to submit to God’s judgements brought by His messengers, seek help against your own soul through patience in cutting off familiar habits and abandoning egoic shares and desires. At the root of their branching growth lies love of leadership and rank; whoever patiently abandons these wins God’s good pleasure. The hadith says, “In patience over what you dislike is much good.” The poet says:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">Patience is like aloes: bitter to the taste,<br>but its consequences are sweeter than honey.<\/blockquote><p>Alternatively: seek help through fasting and prayer. Fasting breaks desire and purifies the soul; when purified of vices, the soul is adorned with virtues such as humility, fairness, reverence and the other noble qualities. Prayer contains spiritual and bodily forms of worship: purification, covering one’s nakedness, spending money on both, facing the Kaʿba, remaining devoted to worship, displaying humility with the limbs, making the heart’s intention sincere, striving against Satan, conversing intimately with the All-Merciful, reciting the Qurʾān, and withholding oneself from the two pleasures.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-44\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B040]<\/a><\/p><p>Through prayer needs are fulfilled and afflictions repaired. Hence, when a matter troubled the Prophet, God bless him and grant him peace, he took refuge in prayer. It is burdensome to the soul because it recurs each day and comes at the hour when sleep is sweet—except for the humble, in whose hearts its sweetness has settled. They converse with their Lord in it until it becomes the delight of their eyes.<\/p><p>They are certain that they will meet their Lord and delight in beholding His noble Face; they are also certain of returning to Him through resurrection and gathering, for reward and punishment. The Real Most High here uses expectation (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الظن<\/bdi>) where certainty is intended out of consideration for sinners and indulgence towards the disobedient, who lack certainty’s purity. Had He mentioned unmixed certainty, they would have been excluded. Glory be to such a forbearing Lord, such a munificent, generous One! God, favour us with pure gnosis and certainty, so that no illusion or doubt stirs in our hearts, Lord of the worlds.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: you who are afflicted by leadership and rank, and are too proud to submit to God’s judgements brought by His messengers, seek help against your own soul through patience in cutting off familiar habits and abandoning egoic shares and desires. At the root of their branching growth lies love of leadership and rank; whoever patiently abandons these wins God’s good pleasure. The hadith says, “In patience over what you dislike is much good.” The poet says: Patience is like aloes: bitter to the taste, but its consequences are sweeter than honey. Alternatively: seek help through fasting and prayer. Fasting breaks desire and purifies the soul; when purified of vices, the soul is adorned with virtues such as humility, fairness, reverence and the other noble qualities. Prayer contains spiritual and bodily forms of worship: purification, covering one’s nakedness, spending money on both, facing the Kaʿba, remaining devoted to worship, displaying humility with the limbs, making the heart’s intention sincere, striving against Satan, conversing intimately with the All-Merciful, reciting the Qurʾān, and withholding oneself from the two pleasures. [B040] Through prayer needs are fulfilled and afflictions repaired. Hence, when a matter troubled the Prophet, God bless him and grant him peace, he took refuge in prayer. It is burdensome to the soul because it recurs each day and comes at the hour when sleep is sweet—except for the humble, in whose hearts its sweetness has settled. They converse with their Lord in it until it becomes the delight of their eyes. They are certain that they will meet their Lord and delight in beholding His noble Face; they are also certain of returning to Him through resurrection and gathering, for reward and punishment. The Real Most High here uses expectation ( الظن ) where certainty is intended out of consideration for sinners and indulgence towards the disobedient, who lack certainty’s purity. Had He mentioned unmixed certainty, they would have been excluded. Glory be to such a forbearing Lord, such a munificent, generous One! God, favour us with pure gnosis and certainty, so that no illusion or doubt stirs in our hearts, Lord of the worlds."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Persevering at the door of the Presence","html":"<p>You who seek entry into God’s Presence, abase and humble yourself before His friends. Swallow the bitterness of patience in this so that they may bring you into His Presence. As the poet says:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">Humble yourself before the one you love: love is not easy.<br>When the beloved is pleased, union is assured.<\/blockquote><p>Should love of leadership and rank prevent you, seek help through patience and prayer. Patience is the sign of victory, and prayer restrains indecency and wrongdoing. Keep knocking at the door until you enter with the beloveds. Perseverance in prayer is a weighty matter, except for one who reaches intimate converse with the Most High, the Great, realizes the meeting of witnessing and direct vision, and returns to his Master at every moment. Prayer then becomes the delight of his eye. Through God is success, and He guides to the even way.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"You who seek entry into God’s Presence, abase and humble yourself before His friends. Swallow the bitterness of patience in this so that they may bring you into His Presence. As the poet says: Humble yourself before the one you love: love is not easy. When the beloved is pleased, union is assured. Should love of leadership and rank prevent you, seek help through patience and prayer. Patience is the sign of victory, and prayer restrains indecency and wrongdoing. Keep knocking at the door until you enter with the beloveds. Perseverance in prayer is a weighty matter, except for one who reaches intimate converse with the Most High, the Great, realizes the meeting of witnessing and direct vision, and returns to his Master at every moment. Prayer then becomes the delight of his eye. Through God is success, and He guides to the even way."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>Having commanded the foundations and branches, He reminds them of His blessings and warns them of the threatened consequence of failing to give thanks, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Having commanded the foundations and branches, He reminds them of His blessings and warns them of the threatened consequence of failing to give thanks, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C102.1","page":102,"ar":"أي: الأكل والجماع. قاله الشهاب الخفاجي في حاشيته على البيضاوي ٢/٤٥١.","en":"That is, eating and sexual intercourse. Al-Shihāb al-Khafājī says this in his gloss on al-Bayḍāwī, 2:451.","anchor":"وكف النفس عن الأطيبين"},{"id":"C102.2","page":102,"ar":"أرى أن يكون: (تذلل لمن تهوى فما في الهوى سهل).","en":"I suggest that it should read: “Humble yourself before the one you love, for nothing in love is easy.”","anchor":"تذلل لمن تهوى فليس الهوى سهل"}],"english_characters":3621,"arabic_characters":2559},{"id":"q2-47-48","q":"2:47–48","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:47–48 · Gratitude for distinction and accountability for influence","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 103–104","cairo":"I:103–104","source_page_first":103,"source_page_last":104,"ar":"يا بَنِي إِسْرائِيلَ اذْكُرُوا نِعْمَتِيَ الَّتِي أَنْعَمْتُ عَلَيْكُمْ وَأَنِّي فَضَّلْتُكُمْ عَلَى الْعالَمِينَ (47) وَاتَّقُوا يَوْماً لا تَجْزِي نَفْسٌ عَنْ نَفْسٍ شَيْئاً وَلا تُقْبَلُ مِنْها شَفاعَةٌ وَلا يُؤْخَذُ مِنْها عَدْلٌ وَلا هُمْ يُنْصَرُونَ (48)\nقلت: العدل بالفتح: الفداء، وبالكسر: الحمل، وجملة لا تَجْزِي: صفة ليوم، والعائد محذوف، أي: لا تجزي فيه.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: يا بَنِي إِسْرائِيلَ اذْكُرُوا نِعْمَتِيَ الَّتِي أَنْعَمْتُ على آبائكم بالهداية وبعث الرسل، وَأَنِّي فَضَّلْتُكُمْ عَلَى الْعالَمِينَ: أهل زمانكم، فاذكروا هذه النعم واشكروني عليها بأن تتبعوا هذا النبيّ الجليل، الذي تجدونه مكتوباً عندكم في التوراة والإنجيل.\nوخافوا يَوْماً لا تَقْضي فيه نَفْسٌ عَنْ نَفْسٍ شَيْئاً بحيث لا تجلِب لها نفعاً، ولا تدفع عنها ضرراً، ولا تقبل مِنْها شَفاعَةٌ إن وقعت الشفاعة فيها، ولا يؤخذ منها فداء، إن أرادت الفداء عنها، ولا تنتصر في دفع العذاب، إن أرادت الانتصار بعشيرتها. فانتفى عنها وجوه الامتناع من العذاب بأي وجه أمكن فإن الإنسان إذا أُخذ للنكال احتال على نفسه إما بالشفاعة، أو بالفداء إن لم تقبل الشفاعة فيه، أو بالانتصار بأقاربه، والآيةُ في الكفار، فلا حجةَ لمن ينفِي الشفاعة في عُصَاة المؤمنين، والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: قد يتوجَّهُ العتاب إلى أهل الرئاسة والجاه، من العلماء والصالحين، وكل من خُصَّ بشرف أو خصوصية، فيقول لهم الحق تعالى: اذْكُرُوا نِعْمَتِيَ الَّتِي أَنْعَمْتُ عَلَيْكُمْ بالعلم أو السيادة أو الصلاح، وبأن فضلتكم على أهل زمانكم، وخصصتكم من أبناء جنسكم فقد رُوِيَ: «أنَّ العبد يُحاسب على جاهه كما يُحاسب على ماله» . فمن صرفه في طاعة الله، وتواضع لعباد الله، وسعى في حوائجهم، وأبلغ الجهد في قضاء مآربهم، كان ذلك شكراً لنعمة الجاه فقد رُوِيَ في الحديث: «مَنْ سَعَى في حَاجَةِ أخيهِ المسْلِم، قُضِيتْ أو لَم تُقْضَ، غُفِر لَه ما تقدَم مِنْ ذنبه، وكُتب له براءتان: براءة من النار وبراءة من النفاق» .\nولا يأخذ على ذلك أجراً ولا جُعلاً فإنَّ ذلك سحت وربا، ومن تكبّر به وطغى، أو أخذ على ذلك أجراً، قيل له يوم القيامة: قد استوفيت أجرك فلا حظ لك عندنا، فلا تنفعه شفاعة، ولا يقبل منه فداء، ولا يقدر أن ينتصر من موارد الهوان والردى، ففي بعض الأخبار: يقول الله تعالى للفقراء الذين يعظمون في الدنيا لأجل فقرهم: ألم أرخص لكم الأسعار؟ ألم أوسع لكم المجالس؟ ألم أُعطِّف عليكم عبادي؟ فقد أخذتم أجركم في الدنيا. أو كما قال.\nوالله تعالى أعلم.\nثم ذكّر الحق تعالى بنى إسرائيل بنعمة أخرى، فقال:","quran_ar":"يا بَنِي إِسْرائِيلَ اذْكُرُوا نِعْمَتِيَ الَّتِي أَنْعَمْتُ عَلَيْكُمْ وَأَنِّي فَضَّلْتُكُمْ عَلَى الْعالَمِينَ (47) وَاتَّقُوا يَوْماً لا تَجْزِي نَفْسٌ عَنْ نَفْسٍ شَيْئاً وَلا تُقْبَلُ مِنْها شَفاعَةٌ وَلا يُؤْخَذُ مِنْها عَدْلٌ وَلا هُمْ يُنْصَرُونَ (48)","quran_en":"47. Children of Israel, remember My blessing which I bestowed upon you, and that I favoured you above the worlds. 48. Guard yourselves against a day when no soul can avail another in anything, no intercession will be accepted from it, no ransom taken from it, and they will not be helped.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Ransom and the omitted resumptive","html":"<p>I say: <i>ʿadl<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عَدل<\/bdi>), with fatḥa, is ransom; <i>ʿidl<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عِدل<\/bdi>), with kasra, is a load. “No soul can avail…” qualifies “a day,” with the resumptive omitted: “on which no soul can avail another.”<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: ʿadl ( عَدل ), with fatḥa, is ransom; ʿidl ( عِدل ), with kasra, is a load. “No soul can avail…” qualifies “a day,” with the resumptive omitted: “on which no soul can avail another.”"},{"type":"theology","title":"The limits of intercession in this verse","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: Children of Israel, remember My blessing upon your fathers through guidance and the sending of messengers, and My favouring you above the worlds—the people of your own time. Remember these blessings and thank Me by following this illustrious Prophet, whom you find recorded with you in the Torah and Gospel.<\/p><p>Fear a day when one soul cannot discharge anything for another, neither securing it benefit nor warding off harm. No intercession is accepted on its behalf, should intercession be attempted; no ransom is taken, should ransom be offered; and it cannot obtain help against punishment by appealing to its clan. Every possible means of resisting punishment is thus excluded. When a person is seized for chastisement, he contrives to escape through intercession; if that is not accepted, through ransom; or through the support of relatives. The verse concerns unbelievers and therefore supplies no proof to those who deny intercession for sinful believers. And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: Children of Israel, remember My blessing upon your fathers through guidance and the sending of messengers, and My favouring you above the worlds—the people of your own time. Remember these blessings and thank Me by following this illustrious Prophet, whom you find recorded with you in the Torah and Gospel. Fear a day when one soul cannot discharge anything for another, neither securing it benefit nor warding off harm. No intercession is accepted on its behalf, should intercession be attempted; no ransom is taken, should ransom be offered; and it cannot obtain help against punishment by appealing to its clan. Every possible means of resisting punishment is thus excluded. When a person is seized for chastisement, he contrives to escape through intercession; if that is not accepted, through ransom; or through the support of relatives. The verse concerns unbelievers and therefore supplies no proof to those who deny intercession for sinful believers. And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The blessing and danger of social standing","html":"<p>This reproach may be directed at scholars, righteous people and others possessing leadership, rank, honour or special distinction. The Real Most High tells them: remember My blessing of knowledge, precedence or righteousness, My preferring you over the people of your time and distinguishing you from your fellows. It is reported: “The servant is called to account for his standing as he is called to account for his wealth.” Whoever uses it in obedience to God, humbles himself before God’s servants, attends to their needs and strives to fulfil their purposes has given thanks for the blessing of influence. A hadith is reported: “Whoever strives to fulfil his Muslim brother’s need, whether it is fulfilled or not, has his past sins forgiven and receives two certificates of freedom: from the Fire and from hypocrisy.”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-45\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B041]<\/a><\/p><p>He takes no wage or payment for this, for such gain is illicit consumption and usury. Whoever grows proud and tyrannical through his influence, or takes payment for it, is told on the Day of Resurrection: “You have already received your wage; you have no share with Us.” Intercession will not benefit him, no ransom will be accepted, and he cannot defend himself against the inroads of humiliation and ruin. In one report, God Most High says to the spiritual poor honoured in this world for their poverty: “Did I not lower prices for you? Did I not make room for you in assemblies? Did I not incline My servants kindly towards you? You have received your reward in the world”—or words to that effect. And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"This reproach may be directed at scholars, righteous people and others possessing leadership, rank, honour or special distinction. The Real Most High tells them: remember My blessing of knowledge, precedence or righteousness, My preferring you over the people of your time and distinguishing you from your fellows. It is reported: “The servant is called to account for his standing as he is called to account for his wealth.” Whoever uses it in obedience to God, humbles himself before God’s servants, attends to their needs and strives to fulfil their purposes has given thanks for the blessing of influence. A hadith is reported: “Whoever strives to fulfil his Muslim brother’s need, whether it is fulfilled or not, has his past sins forgiven and receives two certificates of freedom: from the Fire and from hypocrisy.” [B041] He takes no wage or payment for this, for such gain is illicit consumption and usury. Whoever grows proud and tyrannical through his influence, or takes payment for it, is told on the Day of Resurrection: “You have already received your wage; you have no share with Us.” Intercession will not benefit him, no ransom will be accepted, and he cannot defend himself against the inroads of humiliation and ruin. In one report, God Most High says to the spiritual poor honoured in this world for their poverty: “Did I not lower prices for you? Did I not make room for you in assemblies? Did I not incline My servants kindly towards you? You have received your reward in the world”—or words to that effect. And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real Most High reminds the Children of Israel of another blessing, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real Most High reminds the Children of Israel of another blessing, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":2856,"arabic_characters":2209},{"id":"q2-49","q":"2:49","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:49 · Deliverance from Pharaoh and the tyrannies of every age","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 104–105","cairo":"I:104–105","source_page_first":104,"source_page_last":105,"ar":"وَإِذْ نَجَّيْناكُمْ مِنْ آلِ فِرْعَوْنَ يَسُومُونَكُمْ سُوءَ الْعَذابِ يُذَبِّحُونَ أَبْناءَكُمْ وَيَسْتَحْيُونَ نِساءَكُمْ وَفِي ذلِكُمْ بَلاءٌ مِنْ رَبِّكُمْ عَظِيمٌ (49)\nقلت: (إذ) : معمول لا ذكروا، و (فرعون) : اسم لكل من ملك القبط، كما أن قيصر اسم لمن ملك الروم، وكسرى اسم لمن ملك الفرس، واسم (فرعون) الذي كان في زمن موسى عليه السلام: «مصعب بن ريان» ، وقيل: ابنه الوليد.\nوسَام يسُوم: طلب وبغى، يقال: سامه خسفاً إذا أولاه ظلماً، وجملة (يسومونكم) : حال من (آل فرعون) ، وجملة (يذبحون) : بيان لها. وسوء العذاب: أفظعه وأقبحه.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: يا بني إسرائيل اذكروا نعمة أخرى أنعمت بها على أسلافكم، وأنتم عالمون بها، وذلك حين أنجيناكم من عذاب فرعون ورهطه، يولونكم أقبحَ العذاب وأشنعَه، كانوا يستعبدون رجالكم ونساءكم في مشاق الخدمة والمهنة، ولمّا أخبره الكهان أنه سيخرج منكم ولد يُخَرِّب ملكه، جعل يذبح ذكوركم ويترك نساءكم، وفي ذلكم محنة مِنْ رَبِّكُمْ وابتلاء عَظِيمٌ، أو في ذلك الإنجاء اختبار من ربكم عظيم، فاذكروا هذه النعمة، وتحصنوا بالإيمان بمحمد صلى الله عليه وسلّم من محنة أخرى، ولا ينفع حذرٌ من قدر، وَكانَ أَمْرُ اللَّهِ قَدَراً مَقْدُوراً.\nوبالله التوفيق.\nالإشارة: لكل زمان فراعين وجبابرة يقطعون الناس عن الانقطاع إلى الله والدخول إلى حضرة الله، (ضَلَّ سَعْيُهُمْ فِى الحَيَاةِ الدُّنْيَا وَهُمْ يَحْسَبُونَ أنهم يُحسنون صنعا) ، يقول الحق جلّ جلاله للذين تخلصوا منهم: اذكروا نعمتي التي أنعمت عليكم بها حيث أنجيتكم من فراعين زمنكم، يسومونكم سوء العذاب وهو البقاء في غم الحجاب، والانقطاع عن الأحباب، يقتلون ما ربيتم من اليقين في قلوبكم والمعرفة في أسراركم، ويستحيون\nشهواتكم وحظوظكم، (وفي ذلكم بلاء من ربكم عظيم) . قال تعالى: وَإِنْ تُطِعْ أَكْثَرَ مَنْ فِي الْأَرْضِ يُضِلُّوكَ عَنْ سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ إِنْ يَتَّبِعُونَ إِلَّا الظَّنَّ.... وبالله التوفيق، وهو الهادي إلى سواء الطريق.","quran_ar":"وَإِذْ نَجَّيْناكُمْ مِنْ آلِ فِرْعَوْنَ يَسُومُونَكُمْ سُوءَ الْعَذابِ يُذَبِّحُونَ أَبْناءَكُمْ وَيَسْتَحْيُونَ نِساءَكُمْ وَفِي ذلِكُمْ بَلاءٌ مِنْ رَبِّكُمْ عَظِيمٌ (49)","quran_en":"49. When We delivered you from Pharaoh’s people, who subjected you to grievous torment, slaughtering your sons and sparing your women: in that was a great trial from your Lord.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Pharaoh and the infliction of torment","html":"<p>I say: <i>idh<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">إذ<\/bdi>), “when,” is governed by “remember.” Pharaoh is the title of anyone who ruled the Copts, just as Caesar designates the ruler of the Romans and Chosroes the ruler of the Persians. The Pharaoh in Moses’s time was named Muṣʿab ibn Rayyān, or, according to another account, his son al-Walīd.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-46\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B042]<\/a><\/p><p><i>Sāma, yasūmu<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">سام يسوم<\/bdi>) means to seek or pursue. One says, “He subjected him to abasement,” when he treated him unjustly. “Subjecting you” is circumstantial in relation to “Pharaoh’s people,” and “slaughtering” explains it. Grievous torment is its most appalling and ugly form.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: idh ( إذ ), “when,” is governed by “remember.” Pharaoh is the title of anyone who ruled the Copts, just as Caesar designates the ruler of the Romans and Chosroes the ruler of the Persians. The Pharaoh in Moses’s time was named Muṣʿab ibn Rayyān, or, according to another account, his son al-Walīd. [B042] Sāma, yasūmu ( سام يسوم ) means to seek or pursue. One says, “He subjected him to abasement,” when he treated him unjustly. “Subjecting you” is circumstantial in relation to “Pharaoh’s people,” and “slaughtering” explains it. Grievous torment is its most appalling and ugly form."},{"type":"exposition","title":"A trial in affliction or in deliverance","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: Children of Israel, remember another blessing upon your forebears, known to you: We rescued you from the torment of Pharaoh and his people, who inflicted the ugliest and most terrible suffering upon you. They enslaved your men and women in arduous service and menial work. When the soothsayers told him that a child from among you would destroy his kingdom, he began slaughtering your males and sparing your women. In that was an affliction and mighty trial from your Lord; alternatively, the mighty trial lay in that deliverance. Remember this blessing and fortify yourselves through belief in Muḥammad, God bless him and grant him peace, against another affliction. Caution does not avail against destiny: “God’s command is a determined decree” [33:38]. Through God is success.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: Children of Israel, remember another blessing upon your forebears, known to you: We rescued you from the torment of Pharaoh and his people, who inflicted the ugliest and most terrible suffering upon you. They enslaved your men and women in arduous service and menial work. When the soothsayers told him that a child from among you would destroy his kingdom, he began slaughtering your males and sparing your women. In that was an affliction and mighty trial from your Lord; alternatively, the mighty trial lay in that deliverance. Remember this blessing and fortify yourselves through belief in Muḥammad, God bless him and grant him peace, against another affliction. Caution does not avail against destiny: “God’s command is a determined decree” [33:38]. Through God is success."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Those who obstruct the way to God","html":"<p>Every age has pharaohs and tyrants who prevent people from severing attachments for God’s sake and entering His Presence: “Their striving goes astray in worldly life, while they suppose they do good work” [18:104]. The Real, glorious is His majesty, says to those freed from them: remember My blessing when I rescued you from the pharaohs of your time. They subjected you to grievous torment—remaining in the gloom of the veil and severance from the beloveds. They kill the certainty you have cultivated in your hearts and the gnosis in your innermost selves, while keeping alive<\/p><p>your desires and egoic shares. “In that is a great trial from your Lord.” He says, “If you obey most of those on earth, they will lead you astray from God’s path; they follow only conjecture…” [6:116]. Through God is success, and He guides to the even way.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Every age has pharaohs and tyrants who prevent people from severing attachments for God’s sake and entering His Presence: “Their striving goes astray in worldly life, while they suppose they do good work” [18:104]. The Real, glorious is His majesty, says to those freed from them: remember My blessing when I rescued you from the pharaohs of your time. They subjected you to grievous torment—remaining in the gloom of the veil and severance from the beloveds. They kill the certainty you have cultivated in your hearts and the gnosis in your innermost selves, while keeping alive your desires and egoic shares. “In that is a great trial from your Lord.” He says, “If you obey most of those on earth, they will lead you astray from God’s path; they follow only conjecture…” [6:116]. Through God is success, and He guides to the even way."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":2247,"arabic_characters":1689},{"id":"q2-50","q":"2:50","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:50 · The divided sea and liberation from desire","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 105–105","cairo":"I:105","source_page_first":105,"source_page_last":105,"ar":"وَإِذْ فَرَقْنا بِكُمُ الْبَحْرَ فَأَنْجَيْناكُمْ وَأَغْرَقْنا آلَ فِرْعَوْنَ وَأَنْتُمْ تَنْظُرُونَ (50)\nثم ذكَّرهم الحق تعالى نعمة أخرى وهي فلق البحر وإغراق العدو، فقال:\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: واذكروا أيضاً حين فَرَقْنا بسببكم الْبَحْرَ، حين فررتم من عدوكم، فسلكتم فيه اثنيْ عشر مسلكاً يابساً، حتى خلصتم إلى الشام، فلما أدرككم عدوُّكم، واسْتَتَمَّ دخولهُ فيه، أطبقنا عليهم البحر فَأَنْجَيْناكُمْ وَأَغْرَقْنا آلَ فِرْعَوْنَ وأنتم تعاينون غرقهم وهلاكهم، فاشكروا هذه النعم التي أنعمت بها على أسلافكم، واتبعوا أحسن ما أنزل إليكم من ربكم على نبي أُمي، لم يكن له علم بهذا، حتى علمه بالوحي من ربكم.\nالإشارة: قال بعض الحكماء: (الهوى بحر لا ساحل له إلا الموت) . فلا يقطع بحر الحظوظ والعوائد، إلا الخواص، الذين منَّ الله عليهم بسلوك الطريقة، والغرق في بحر الحقيقة، على يد رجال جمعوا بين الشريعة والحقيقة، فيقول الحق- جلّ جلاله- لمن تخلَّص من بحر هواه، وأفضى إلى مشاهدة مولاه: اذكروا نعمتي التي أنعمت عليكم حيث خلصتكم من بحر الشهوات والعوائد، وأطلعتكم على أسرار العلوم وذخائر الفوائد، وأغرقنا فيه من تكبر وطغى، وأنت تنظرون ما فيه الناس من غم الحجاب وسوء الحساب، في بحر لجى يغشاه موج الذنوب، من فوقه موج الحظوظ، من فوقه سحاب الأثر، إذا أخرج يده لم يكد يراها ومن لم يجعل اللهُ لَهُ نُورًا فَمَا له من نورٍ. وبالله التوفيق.\nثم ذكّرهم نعمة التوراة التي أنزلها على موسى، وفى ضمنه التوبيخ على عبادة العجل، فقال جل جلاله:","quran_ar":"وَإِذْ فَرَقْنا بِكُمُ الْبَحْرَ فَأَنْجَيْناكُمْ وَأَغْرَقْنا آلَ فِرْعَوْنَ وَأَنْتُمْ تَنْظُرُونَ (50)","quran_en":"50. When We divided the sea for you, delivered you, and drowned Pharaoh’s people while you looked on.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Deliverance witnessed","html":"<p>The Real Most High reminds them of another blessing: the dividing of the sea and drowning of the enemy. He says:<\/p><p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: remember also when We divided the sea for your sake as you fled your enemy. You travelled through twelve dry pathways until you escaped towards Syria. When your enemy overtook you and had fully entered, We closed the sea upon them, delivering you and drowning Pharaoh’s people while you watched their drowning and destruction. Give thanks for these blessings upon your forebears and follow the best of what your Lord has sent down to you upon an unlettered Prophet. He had no knowledge of this until your Lord taught him through revelation.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real Most High reminds them of another blessing: the dividing of the sea and drowning of the enemy. He says: The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: remember also when We divided the sea for your sake as you fled your enemy. You travelled through twelve dry pathways until you escaped towards Syria. When your enemy overtook you and had fully entered, We closed the sea upon them, delivering you and drowning Pharaoh’s people while you watched their drowning and destruction. Give thanks for these blessings upon your forebears and follow the best of what your Lord has sent down to you upon an unlettered Prophet. He had no knowledge of this until your Lord taught him through revelation."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Crossing the sea of customary attachments","html":"<p>One of the sages said: “Caprice is an ocean whose only shore is death.” Only the elect cross the sea of egoic shares and habitual attachments: those whom God has favoured with travelling the Path and immersion in the ocean of Reality, at the hands of men who unite the Sacred Law and Reality. To one freed from the sea of his caprice and brought to witnessing his Master, the Real, glorious is His majesty, says: remember My blessing when I freed you from the sea of desires and habits, disclosed to you the mysteries of the sciences and treasures of benefits, and drowned in it those who grew proud and transgressed. You see people suffering the gloom of the veil and an evil reckoning, in a deep sea covered by the wave of sins, above it the wave of egoic shares, and above that the cloud of created effects. When one stretches out his hand, he can scarcely see it: “He for whom God appoints no light has no light” [24:40]. Through God is success.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"One of the sages said: “Caprice is an ocean whose only shore is death.” Only the elect cross the sea of egoic shares and habitual attachments: those whom God has favoured with travelling the Path and immersion in the ocean of Reality, at the hands of men who unite the Sacred Law and Reality. To one freed from the sea of his caprice and brought to witnessing his Master, the Real, glorious is His majesty, says: remember My blessing when I freed you from the sea of desires and habits, disclosed to you the mysteries of the sciences and treasures of benefits, and drowned in it those who grew proud and transgressed. You see people suffering the gloom of the veil and an evil reckoning, in a deep sea covered by the wave of sins, above it the wave of egoic shares, and above that the cloud of created effects. When one stretches out his hand, he can scarcely see it: “He for whom God appoints no light has no light” [24:40]. Through God is success."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then reminds them of the blessing of the Torah sent down upon Moses, including within this a rebuke for worshipping the calf, saying, glorious is His majesty:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then reminds them of the blessing of the Torah sent down upon Moses, including within this a rebuke for worshipping the calf, saying, glorious is His majesty:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":1804,"arabic_characters":1304},{"id":"q2-51-53","q":"2:51–53","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:51–53 · The forty nights and fidelity during the guide’s absence","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 105–106","cairo":"I:105–106","source_page_first":105,"source_page_last":106,"ar":"وَإِذْ واعَدْنا مُوسى أَرْبَعِينَ لَيْلَةً ثُمَّ اتَّخَذْتُمُ الْعِجْلَ مِنْ بَعْدِهِ وَأَنْتُمْ ظالِمُونَ (51) ثُمَّ عَفَوْنا عَنْكُمْ مِنْ بَعْدِ ذلِكَ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُونَ (52) وَإِذْ آتَيْنا مُوسَى الْكِتابَ وَالْفُرْقانَ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَهْتَدُونَ (53)\nقلت: (أربعين) : مفعول لواعدنا، لا ظرف، و (العجل) : مفعول أول، والثاني محذوف، أي: اتخذتموه إلها، و (الفرقان) : معطوف على (الكتاب) .\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: واذكروا أيضاً حين واعَدْنا مُوسى أن يصوم أَرْبَعِينَ لَيْلَةً بأيامها متواصلة، وذلك حين طلبتم منه أن ينزل عليه الكتاب فيه بيان الأحكام، ثم لما صامها، وهي: ذو القعدة وعشر ذي الحجة، وأتى إلى المناجاة، كفرتم، واتَّخَذْتُمُ الْعِجْلَ الذي صاغه السامِريُّ من الحُليّ، الذي أخذته نساء بني إسرائيل من القبط عارية، ففرّوا به ظنّاً منهم أنه حلال، فقال لهم هارون عليه السلام: لا يحل لكم، فطرحوه في حفرة، فصاغ منه السامري صورة العجل، وألقى في جوفه قبضة أخذها من تحت حافر فرس جبريل عليه السلام حين عبر معهم البحر، فجعل يخور، فقال السامري: هذا إِلهُكُمْ وَإِلهُ مُوسى، وَأَنْتُمْ ظالِمُونَ في عبادته، ثُمَّ عَفَوْنا عَنْكُمْ بالتوبة وقتل النفس على ما يأتي، لَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُونَ، فلا تعصون بنعمة، وَاذكروا أيضاً إِذْ آتَيْنا مُوسَى الْكِتابَ الذي طلبتم، وهو التوراة، وهو الْفُرْقانَ الذي فرقنا فيه بين الحق والباطل، كي تهتدوا إلى الصواب فتنجوا من العذاب.\nالإشارة: ما زالت الأشياخ والأولياء الأقدمون ينتحلون طريق سيدنا موسى عليه السلام في استعمال هذه الأربعين، ينفردون فيها إلى مولاهم، مؤانسة ومناجاة، وفي ذلك يقول ابن الفارض رضى الله عنه:\nوصِرْتُ مُوسَى زَمَاني ... مذ صارَ بَعْضِيَ كُلِّي\nوقال:\nصارتْ جِبالِيَ دكًا ... مِنْ هَيْبَةِ المُتَجَلِّي\nفيفارقون عشائرهم وأصحابهم في مناجاة الحبيب، والمؤانسة بالقريب، فمن أصحابهم مَن يبقى على عهده في حال غيبة شيخه، من المجاهدة والمشاهدة، ومنهم مَن تسرقه العاجلة فيرجع إلى عبادة عجل حظه وهواه فيظلم نفسه بمتابعة دنياه، فإن بادر بالتوبة والإقلاع، ورجع إلى حضرة شيخه بالاستماع والاتباع، وقع عنه العفو والغفران، ورجا ما كان يؤمله من المشاهدة والعيان، وإلا باء بالعقوبة والخسران، وكل مَن اعتزل عن الأحباب والعشائر والأصحاب، طالباً جمع قلبه، ورضى ربه، فلا بد أن ترد عليه أسرار ربانية ومواهب لدنية، من لدن حكيم عليم، يظهر بها الحق، ويدفع بها الباطل، فيفرق بين الحق والباطل. والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم ذكر الحق تعالى كيفية توبة من عبد العجل منهم، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَإِذْ واعَدْنا مُوسى أَرْبَعِينَ لَيْلَةً ثُمَّ اتَّخَذْتُمُ الْعِجْلَ مِنْ بَعْدِهِ وَأَنْتُمْ ظالِمُونَ (51) ثُمَّ عَفَوْنا عَنْكُمْ مِنْ بَعْدِ ذلِكَ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُونَ (52) وَإِذْ آتَيْنا مُوسَى الْكِتابَ وَالْفُرْقانَ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَهْتَدُونَ (53)","quran_en":"51. When We appointed forty nights for Moses; then, after his departure, you took the calf for worship, and you were wrongdoers. 52. Yet We pardoned you after that, so that you might give thanks. 53. When We gave Moses the Book and the Criterion, so that you might be guided.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The appointed period and the omitted object","html":"<p>I say: “forty” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أربعين<\/bdi>) is the object of “We appointed,” not an adverbial expression. “The calf” is the first object, while the second is omitted: “you took it as a god.” “The Criterion” is coordinated with “the Book.”<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “forty” ( أربعين ) is the object of “We appointed,” not an adverbial expression. “The calf” is the first object, while the second is omitted: “you took it as a god.” “The Criterion” is coordinated with “the Book.”"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The calf and the gift of the Torah","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: remember when We appointed for Moses forty consecutive nights, with their days, to fast. This followed your request that a Book explaining the judgements be sent down upon him. When he had fasted them—Dhū al-Qaʿda and ten days of Dhū al-Ḥijja—and came to intimate converse, you disbelieved and took the calf for worship. Al-Sāmirī had fashioned it from jewellery borrowed by the Israelite women from the Copts. They fled with it, supposing it lawful. Aaron, peace be upon him, told them, “It is not lawful for you,” so they cast it into a pit. Al-Sāmirī shaped it into a calf and cast into its hollow a handful taken from beneath the hoof of Gabriel’s horse when he crossed the sea with them. It began to low. Al-Sāmirī said, “This is your god and Moses’s god” [20:88]. You wronged yourselves by worshipping it.<\/p><p>Then We pardoned you through repentance and the killing that will be explained, so that you might give thanks and not use a blessing to disobey. Remember also when We gave Moses the Book you requested, the Torah: it is the Criterion by which We distinguished truth from falsehood, so that you might be guided to what is right and escape punishment.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: remember when We appointed for Moses forty consecutive nights, with their days, to fast. This followed your request that a Book explaining the judgements be sent down upon him. When he had fasted them—Dhū al-Qaʿda and ten days of Dhū al-Ḥijja—and came to intimate converse, you disbelieved and took the calf for worship. Al-Sāmirī had fashioned it from jewellery borrowed by the Israelite women from the Copts. They fled with it, supposing it lawful. Aaron, peace be upon him, told them, “It is not lawful for you,” so they cast it into a pit. Al-Sāmirī shaped it into a calf and cast into its hollow a handful taken from beneath the hoof of Gabriel’s horse when he crossed the sea with them. It began to low. Al-Sāmirī said, “This is your god and Moses’s god” [20:88]. You wronged yourselves by worshipping it. Then We pardoned you through repentance and the killing that will be explained, so that you might give thanks and not use a blessing to disobey. Remember also when We gave Moses the Book you requested, the Torah: it is the Criterion by which We distinguished truth from falsehood, so that you might be guided to what is right and escape punishment."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Forty-day withdrawal and the disciple’s fidelity","html":"<p>The early shaykhs and friends of God have continued to follow our master Moses’s path in observing these forty days, withdrawing to their Master in intimacy and converse. In this connection Ibn al-Fāriḍ, God be pleased with him, says:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">I became the Moses of my time<br>when my part became my whole.<\/blockquote><p>And:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">My mountains became level dust<br>in awe of the One disclosing Himself.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-47\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B043]<\/a><\/blockquote><p>They leave their families and companions to converse with the Beloved and find intimacy with the Near One. Some companions remain faithful in their shaykh’s absence, continuing their striving and witnessing. Others are stolen away by the immediate world and return to worshipping the calf of their own share and desire, wronging themselves through following worldly life. If such a person hastens to repent and desist, returning to his shaykh’s presence with listening and obedience, pardon and forgiveness come to him, and he may hope again for the witnessing and direct vision he sought. Otherwise he incurs punishment and loss.<\/p><p>Whoever withdraws from beloveds, family and companions, seeking the gathering of his heart and his Lord’s pleasure, necessarily receives divine mysteries and directly bestowed gifts from a Wise and Knowing One. Through these he manifests truth, repels falsehood, and distinguishes the two. And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"The early shaykhs and friends of God have continued to follow our master Moses’s path in observing these forty days, withdrawing to their Master in intimacy and converse. In this connection Ibn al-Fāriḍ, God be pleased with him, says: I became the Moses of my time when my part became my whole. And: My mountains became level dust in awe of the One disclosing Himself. [B043] They leave their families and companions to converse with the Beloved and find intimacy with the Near One. Some companions remain faithful in their shaykh’s absence, continuing their striving and witnessing. Others are stolen away by the immediate world and return to worshipping the calf of their own share and desire, wronging themselves through following worldly life. If such a person hastens to repent and desist, returning to his shaykh’s presence with listening and obedience, pardon and forgiveness come to him, and he may hope again for the witnessing and direct vision he sought. Otherwise he incurs punishment and loss. Whoever withdraws from beloveds, family and companions, seeking the gathering of his heart and his Lord’s pleasure, necessarily receives divine mysteries and directly bestowed gifts from a Wise and Knowing One. Through these he manifests truth, repels falsehood, and distinguishes the two. And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real Most High explains how those who worshipped the calf repented, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real Most High explains how those who worshipped the calf repented, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":2826,"arabic_characters":2180},{"id":"q2-54","q":"2:54","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:54 · Repentance and the four deaths of the lower self","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 106–108","cairo":"I:106–108","source_page_first":106,"source_page_last":108,"ar":"وَإِذْ قالَ مُوسى لِقَوْمِهِ يا قَوْمِ إِنَّكُمْ ظَلَمْتُمْ أَنْفُسَكُمْ بِاتِّخاذِكُمُ الْعِجْلَ فَتُوبُوا إِلى بارِئِكُمْ فَاقْتُلُوا أَنْفُسَكُمْ ذلِكُمْ خَيْرٌ لَكُمْ عِنْدَ بارِئِكُمْ فَتابَ عَلَيْكُمْ إِنَّهُ هُوَ التَّوَّابُ الرَّحِيمُ (54)\nقلت: البارئ هو: المقدر للأشياء والمظهر لها.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: واذكروا يا بني إسرائيل حين قالَ موسى لِقَوْمِهِ لما رجع من الطور، ووجدهم قد عبدوا العجل: يَا قَوْمِ إِنَّكُمْ ظَلَمْتُمْ أَنْفُسَكُمْ وبخستموها بِاتِّخاذِكُمُ الْعِجْلَ إلاهكم، فَتُوبُوا إِلى خالقكم الذي صوركم في أحسن تقويم، فَاقْتُلُوا أَنْفُسَكُمْ بهدم هذه البنية التي ركبتها في أحسن صورة، فبخستموها، ولم تعرفوا قدرها، فعبدتم أبلد الحيوان، الذي هو البقرة. من لم يعرف حق النعمة فحقيق أن تُسترد منه.\nفذلكم القتل والمبادرة إلى التوبة خَيْرٌ لَكُمْ عِندَ خالقكم، لأنه يفضي إلى الحياة الدائمة والبهجة السرمدية، فلما صعب عليكم القتل للشفقة على الأخ أو القريب، ألقينا عليكم ضبابة حتى أظلم المكان، فاقتتلتم من الغداة إلى العشي، فدعا موسى وهارون- عليهما السلام- بالكشف عنهم، فرفعت السحابة، وقد قُتل سبعون ألفاً، ففعلتم ذلك القتل، فتاب الحق تعالى عليكم، فقبل توبة مَن بقي منكم، وعفا عمن مات إِنَّهُ هُوَ التَّوَّابُ الرَّحِيمُ أي: كثير التوفيق للتوبة، أو كثير قبولها، الرحيم بعباده المؤمنين.\nالإشارة: ما قاله سيدنا موسى عليه السلام لقومه، يقال مثله لمن عبد هواه، وعكف على متابعة دنياه: يا من بخس نفسه بإرخاء العنان في متابعة هواها، حتى حرمها من مشاهدة جمال مولاها، تُب إلى ربك، وانتبه من غفلتك، واقتل نفسك بمخالفة هواها، فلعلها تحيا بمشاهدة مولاها، فما دامت النفس موجودة، وحظوظها لديها مشهودة، وآمالها ممدودة، كيف تطمع أن تدخل حضرة الله، وتتمتع بشهود جماله وسناه؟!\nإنْ تُرِدَ وَصْلَنَا فَمَوْتكَ شَرْطٌ ... لا يَنَالُ الوِصَالَ مَنْ فيهِ فَضْلَهْ(1)\nوقال الحلاجُ في هذا المعنى:\nلَمْ أُسْلِم النفسَ للأسقامِ تُتْلِفُها ... إلاَّ لِعِلْمِي بأَنَّ الوصْلَ يُحْيِيهَا\nوقال أيضاً:\nأُقتُلوني يا ثقاتي ... إِنَّ في قَتْلِي حَياتِي\nوحَيَاتي في مَماتِي ... وممَاتي في حَياتي\nأنا عندي: مَحْوُ ذَاتي ... من أجَلِّ المكْرُمَاتِ\nوبَقَائِي في صِفاتي ... مِنْ قبيح السيّئات\nوقال أيضاً:\nإنْ كان سَفْكُ دَمي أقْصَى مُرادِكُمُ ... فَمَا غَلَتْ نظرةٌ مِنكُم بِسَفْك دَمِي\nوقال الشيخ أبو العباس المرسي رضى الله عنه: (لا يدخل على الله إلا من بابين، أحدهما: الموت الحسي، وهو الموت الطبيعي، والآخر: الموت الذي تعنيه هذه الطائفة) . هـ. وهو موت النفوس، فمن لم تمت نفسه لم تَحْيَى روحه.\nوقال بعض العارفين: (لا يحصل الدخول على الله حتى يموت أربع موتات: موت أحمر، وموت أسود، وموت أبيض، وموت أخضر. أما الموت الأحمر فهو مخالفة الهوى، وأما الموت الأسود فهو تحمل الأذى، وأما الموت الأبيض فهو الجوع- أي: المتوسط- وأما الموت الأخضر فهو لبس المرقعات، وطرح الرقاع بعضها على بعض) .\nقلت: ورأس الهوى وعنصره هو حب الجاه وطلب الرئاسة. فمن نزل إلى أرض الخمول، وخرق عوائد نفسه فيه، انخرقت له الحجب، ولاحت له الأنوار، وأشرقت عليه الأسرار في مدة قريبة، وبالله التوفيق وهو الهادي إلى سواء الطريق.\nثم وبخهم الحق تعالى على طلب الرؤية قبل إبانها، وقبل تحصيل شروطها، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَإِذْ قالَ مُوسى لِقَوْمِهِ يا قَوْمِ إِنَّكُمْ ظَلَمْتُمْ أَنْفُسَكُمْ بِاتِّخاذِكُمُ الْعِجْلَ فَتُوبُوا إِلى بارِئِكُمْ فَاقْتُلُوا أَنْفُسَكُمْ ذلِكُمْ خَيْرٌ لَكُمْ عِنْدَ بارِئِكُمْ فَتابَ عَلَيْكُمْ إِنَّهُ هُوَ التَّوَّابُ الرَّحِيمُ (54)","quran_en":"54. When Moses said to his people, “My people, you have wronged yourselves by taking the calf for worship. Turn in repentance to your Maker and slay one another. That is better for you with your Maker.” Then He turned towards you in mercy. He is indeed the Ever-Relenting, the Merciful.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The Maker","html":"<p>I say: the Maker (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">البارئ<\/bdi>) is He who determines things and brings them forth.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: the Maker ( البارئ ) is He who determines things and brings them forth."},{"type":"exposition","title":"The prescribed repentance in Moses’s community","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: Children of Israel, remember when Moses returned from the Mount, found his people worshipping the calf, and told them: “My people, you have wronged and debased yourselves by taking the calf as your god. Repent to your Creator, who fashioned you in the finest form, and kill one another”—destroying this bodily structure I fashioned so beautifully, but which you debased by failing to recognize its worth and worshipping the dullest animal, the cow. One who fails to recognize the right of a blessing deserves to have it withdrawn.<\/p><p>That killing and haste to repent are better for you in your Creator’s sight, because they lead to everlasting life and perpetual joy. When killing became difficult through compassion for brother or kinsman, We cast a mist over you until the place grew dark. You fought one another from morning to evening. Moses and Aaron, peace be upon them, prayed that the cloud be lifted; it was raised when seventy thousand had been killed. You carried out the killing, and the Real Most High turned towards you, accepting the repentance of those remaining and pardoning the dead. He is the Ever-Relenting—abundant in enabling repentance or accepting it—and merciful towards His believing servants.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: Children of Israel, remember when Moses returned from the Mount, found his people worshipping the calf, and told them: “My people, you have wronged and debased yourselves by taking the calf as your god. Repent to your Creator, who fashioned you in the finest form, and kill one another”—destroying this bodily structure I fashioned so beautifully, but which you debased by failing to recognize its worth and worshipping the dullest animal, the cow. One who fails to recognize the right of a blessing deserves to have it withdrawn. That killing and haste to repent are better for you in your Creator’s sight, because they lead to everlasting life and perpetual joy. When killing became difficult through compassion for brother or kinsman, We cast a mist over you until the place grew dark. You fought one another from morning to evening. Moses and Aaron, peace be upon them, prayed that the cloud be lifted; it was raised when seventy thousand had been killed. You carried out the killing, and the Real Most High turned towards you, accepting the repentance of those remaining and pardoning the dead. He is the Ever-Relenting—abundant in enabling repentance or accepting it—and merciful towards His believing servants."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Dying to caprice, not destroying the body","html":"<p>What our master Moses told his people is likewise said to someone who worships his caprice and persists in following his worldly desire: you who have debased your soul by giving it free rein to follow its inclination, depriving it of witnessing its Master’s beauty, repent to your Lord and awaken from heedlessness. Kill your lower self by opposing its desire, so that it may live through witnessing its Master. So long as the lower self remains present, its shares held in view and its hopes extended, how can you expect to enter God’s Presence and delight in witnessing His beauty and radiance?<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-48\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B044]<\/a><\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">If you desire union with Us, your death is the condition:<br>one in whom a remnant remains does not attain union.<\/blockquote><p>Al-Ḥallāj says in this sense:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">I only surrendered the soul to consuming sicknesses<br>because I knew that union would revive it.<\/blockquote><p>And also:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">Kill me, my trusted friends:<br>in my killing lies my life.<br>My life is in my death,<br>and my death is in my life.<br>The effacing of my self, to me,<br>is among the greatest acts of generosity;<br>my remaining in my own attributes<br>is among the ugly wrongs.<\/blockquote><p>He also says:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">If shedding my blood is your utmost desire,<br>a glance from you is not dearly bought by shedding it.<\/blockquote><p>Shaykh Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Mursī, God be pleased with him, says: “God is entered upon through only two doors: sensory death, which is natural death, and the death intended by this company.” End of quotation. It is the death of the lower selves: one whose lower self has not died has no living spirit.<\/p><p>One of the gnostics said: “Entry unto God is not attained until one has died four deaths: red, black, white and green. Red death is opposing caprice; black death is enduring injury; white death is hunger”—meaning moderate hunger—“and green death is wearing patched garments, laying patches one upon another.”<\/p><p>I say: the head and root of caprice is love of rank and pursuit of leadership. Whoever descends to the earth of obscurity and breaks his soul’s customary habits there has the veils torn apart, the lights disclosed, and the mysteries shine upon him in a short time. Through God is success, and He guides to the even way.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"What our master Moses told his people is likewise said to someone who worships his caprice and persists in following his worldly desire: you who have debased your soul by giving it free rein to follow its inclination, depriving it of witnessing its Master’s beauty, repent to your Lord and awaken from heedlessness. Kill your lower self by opposing its desire, so that it may live through witnessing its Master. So long as the lower self remains present, its shares held in view and its hopes extended, how can you expect to enter God’s Presence and delight in witnessing His beauty and radiance? [B044] If you desire union with Us, your death is the condition: one in whom a remnant remains does not attain union. Al-Ḥallāj says in this sense: I only surrendered the soul to consuming sicknesses because I knew that union would revive it. And also: Kill me, my trusted friends: in my killing lies my life. My life is in my death, and my death is in my life. The effacing of my self, to me, is among the greatest acts of generosity; my remaining in my own attributes is among the ugly wrongs. He also says: If shedding my blood is your utmost desire, a glance from you is not dearly bought by shedding it. Shaykh Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Mursī, God be pleased with him, says: “God is entered upon through only two doors: sensory death, which is natural death, and the death intended by this company.” End of quotation. It is the death of the lower selves: one whose lower self has not died has no living spirit. One of the gnostics said: “Entry unto God is not attained until one has died four deaths: red, black, white and green. Red death is opposing caprice; black death is enduring injury; white death is hunger”—meaning moderate hunger—“and green death is wearing patched garments, laying patches one upon another.” I say: the head and root of caprice is love of rank and pursuit of leadership. Whoever descends to the earth of obscurity and breaks his soul’s customary habits there has the veils torn apart, the lights disclosed, and the mysteries shine upon him in a short time. Through God is success, and He guides to the even way."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real Most High rebukes them for demanding the vision before its time and before fulfilling its conditions, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real Most High rebukes them for demanding the vision before its time and before fulfilling its conditions, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C107.1","page":107,"ar":"البيت للششتري.","en":"The verse is by al-Shushtarī.","anchor":"إن ترد وصلنا فموتك شرط لا ينال الوصال من فيه فضله"}],"english_characters":3586,"arabic_characters":2827},{"id":"q2-55-56","q":"2:55–56","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:55–56 · The thunderbolt and revival after effacement","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 108–109","cairo":"I:108–109","source_page_first":108,"source_page_last":109,"ar":"وَإِذْ قُلْتُمْ يَا مُوسى لَنْ نُؤْمِنَ لَكَ حَتَّى نَرَى اللَّهَ جَهْرَةً فَأَخَذَتْكُمُ الصَّاعِقَةُ وَأَنْتُمْ تَنْظُرُونَ (55) ثُمَّ بَعَثْناكُمْ مِنْ بَعْدِ مَوْتِكُمْ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُونَ (56)\nقلت: (جهرة) : مصدر نرى لأنه نوع منه، أي: نرى الله رؤية عيان، أو حال من الفاعل أي: نراه معاينين له، أو من المفعول أي: نراه معاينة.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: واذكروا أيضاً، يا بني إسرائيل، حين قلتم لموسى عليه السلام لما رجع من الطور، ووجدكم قد عبدتم العجل، فأخذ منكم سبعين رجلاً ممن لم يعبد العجل، وذهب يعتذر، فلما سمعتم كلامي أنكرتموه وحرفتموه، وقلتم: لَنْ نُؤْمِنَ لَكَ أن هذا كلام الله حَتَّى نَرَى اللَّهَ جَهْرَةً فَأَخَذَتْكُمُ الصَّاعِقَةُ بسبب طلبكم ما لا طاقة لكم به، فغبتم عن إحساسكم، وذهبت أرواحكم، وَأَنْتُمْ تَنْظُرُونَ ما فعل بكم، فاستشفع فيكم موسى عليه السلام وقال: يا رَبِّ لَوْ شِئْتَ أَهْلَكْتَهُمْ مِنْ قَبْلُ وَإِيَّايَ أَتُهْلِكُنا بِما فَعَلَ السُّفَهاءُ مِنَّا، كيف أرجع إلى قومي بغير هؤلاء؟ ثُمَّ بَعَثْناكُمْ مِنْ بَعْدِ مَوْتِكُمْ، وعشتم زماناً بعد ذلك لَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُونَ هذه النعمة، وتقومون بحسن الخدمة، فتقروا بربوبيتي، وتصدقوا برسلي، فلم تفعلوا.\nالإشارة: من شأن الأرواح الطيبة التشوق إلى الحضرة، والتشوف إلى العيان والنظرة، فلا يحصل لها كمال التصديق والإيقان إلا بعد الشهود والعيان، فلما علم الحق سبحانه من بعض الأرواح صدق الطلب، رفع عنها الحجاب، وفتح لها الباب، فأخذتها صاعقة الدهشة والحيرة، ولم تطق صدمة المشاهدة والنظرة، فغابت عن الأشكال والرسوم في مشاهدة أنوار الحي القيوم، ثم مَنَّ عليها بالبعث من موت الفناء إلى حياة البقاء، فأمنت من الشقاء، فحصلت لها الحياة الدائمة والسعادة السرمدية. فالصاعقة عند أهل الفن هي عبارة عن الغيبة عن النفس، وفناء دائرة الحس، وهي شهود عدمك لوجود الحق، والبعث منها هو مقام البقاء، وهو شهود الأثر بالله. وهو مقام حق اليقين. وحاصله: شهود وجود الحق وحده، لا عدمك ولا وجودك، «كان الله ولا شيء معه، وهو الآن على ما عليه كان» . وبالله التوفيق.\nثم ذكّرهم الحق لطفه بهم فى حال التيه، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَإِذْ قُلْتُمْ يَا مُوسى لَنْ نُؤْمِنَ لَكَ حَتَّى نَرَى اللَّهَ جَهْرَةً فَأَخَذَتْكُمُ الصَّاعِقَةُ وَأَنْتُمْ تَنْظُرُونَ (55) ثُمَّ بَعَثْناكُمْ مِنْ بَعْدِ مَوْتِكُمْ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُونَ (56)","quran_en":"55. When you said, “Moses, we shall not believe you until we see God openly,” the thunderbolt seized you while you looked on. 56. Then We raised you after your death, so that you might give thanks.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Open vision","html":"<p>I say: “openly” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">جهرة<\/bdi>) functions as the verbal noun of “we see,” because it denotes one kind of seeing: “we see God with direct vision.” Alternatively, it is a circumstantial expression referring to the agent—seeing Him face to face—or to the object—seeing Him openly presented to vision.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “openly” ( جهرة ) functions as the verbal noun of “we see,” because it denotes one kind of seeing: “we see God with direct vision.” Alternatively, it is a circumstantial expression referring to the agent—seeing Him face to face—or to the object—seeing Him openly presented to vision."},{"type":"exposition","title":"The demand for sight and Moses’s intercession","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: Children of Israel, remember what you told Moses after he returned from the Mount and found you worshipping the calf. He chose seventy men who had not worshipped it and went to offer an apology. When you heard My speech, you denied and distorted it, saying, “We shall not believe your claim that this is God’s speech until we see God openly.” The thunderbolt seized you because you demanded what you lacked the capacity to bear. You lost sensory awareness and your spirits departed while you witnessed what was done to you. Moses interceded for you: “Lord, had You willed, You could have destroyed them and me before. Will You destroy us for what the foolish among us have done?” [7:155]. How could he return to his people without them? We then raised you after your death, and you lived for a time thereafter, so that you might thank Us for this blessing and render good service, acknowledging My lordship and affirming My messengers. But you did not.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: Children of Israel, remember what you told Moses after he returned from the Mount and found you worshipping the calf. He chose seventy men who had not worshipped it and went to offer an apology. When you heard My speech, you denied and distorted it, saying, “We shall not believe your claim that this is God’s speech until we see God openly.” The thunderbolt seized you because you demanded what you lacked the capacity to bear. You lost sensory awareness and your spirits departed while you witnessed what was done to you. Moses interceded for you: “Lord, had You willed, You could have destroyed them and me before. Will You destroy us for what the foolish among us have done?” [7:155]. How could he return to his people without them? We then raised you after your death, and you lived for a time thereafter, so that you might thank Us for this blessing and render good service, acknowledging My lordship and affirming My messengers. But you did not."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Effacement and the truth of certainty","html":"<p>Pure spirits naturally long for the Presence and yearn for direct sight and vision. They attain complete affirmation and certainty only after witnessing and direct vision. When the Real—glory be to Him—knows the sincerity of some spirits’ seeking, He raises the veil and opens the door. The thunderbolt of amazement and bewilderment seizes them: unable to bear the shock of witnessing and vision, they are absent from forms and outward traces in witnessing the lights of the Living, the Self-Subsisting. He then favours them with resurrection from the death of effacement (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الفناء<\/bdi>) to the life of subsistence (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">البقاء<\/bdi>). Secure from misery, they attain enduring life and everlasting felicity.<\/p><p>For the people of this art, the thunderbolt signifies absence from the self and the passing away of the sphere of sense perception: witnessing your non-being before the existence of the Real. Resurrection from it is the station of subsistence, witnessing the created effect through God. This is the station of the truth of certainty (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حق اليقين<\/bdi>): its outcome is witnessing the existence of the Real alone, neither your non-being nor your being. “God was, and nothing was with Him; He is now as He was.” Through God is success.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Pure spirits naturally long for the Presence and yearn for direct sight and vision. They attain complete affirmation and certainty only after witnessing and direct vision. When the Real—glory be to Him—knows the sincerity of some spirits’ seeking, He raises the veil and opens the door. The thunderbolt of amazement and bewilderment seizes them: unable to bear the shock of witnessing and vision, they are absent from forms and outward traces in witnessing the lights of the Living, the Self-Subsisting. He then favours them with resurrection from the death of effacement ( الفناء ) to the life of subsistence ( البقاء ). Secure from misery, they attain enduring life and everlasting felicity. For the people of this art, the thunderbolt signifies absence from the self and the passing away of the sphere of sense perception: witnessing your non-being before the existence of the Real. Resurrection from it is the station of subsistence, witnessing the created effect through God. This is the station of the truth of certainty ( حق اليقين ): its outcome is witnessing the existence of the Real alone, neither your non-being nor your being. “God was, and nothing was with Him; He is now as He was.” Through God is success."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real reminds them of His kindness during their wandering, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real reminds them of His kindness during their wandering, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":2573,"arabic_characters":1844},{"id":"q2-57","q":"2:57","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:57 · Manna, quails and the two gardens of the spirit","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 109–110","cairo":"I:109–110","source_page_first":109,"source_page_last":110,"ar":"وَظَلَّلْنا عَلَيْكُمُ الْغَمامَ وَأَنْزَلْنا عَلَيْكُمُ الْمَنَّ وَالسَّلْوى كُلُوا مِنْ طَيِّباتِ ما رَزَقْناكُمْ وَما ظَلَمُونا وَلكِنْ كانُوا أَنْفُسَهُمْ يَظْلِمُونَ (57)\nقلت: (الغّمّامّ) : السحاب الرقيق، و (المَنِّ) هنا: العسل، و (والسّلوى) قيل: اللحم، والأصح: أنه اسم طائر كالسماني.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله في تذكير بني إسرائيل ما أنعم به عليهم في حال التيه: وَقد ظَلَّلْنا عَلَيْكُمُ الْغَمامَ يقيكم من الحر في أيام التيه، وَأَنْزَلْنا عَلَيْكُمُ الْمَنَّ وهو عسل كان ينزل على الشجر من الفجر إلى الطلوع، فيغرفون منه ما شاءوا، وَأنزلنا عليكم السَّلْوى، وهو طير كانت تحشره الجنوب، فينزل عليهم، فياخذون منه ما شاءوا، ولا يمتنع منهم، فيذبحون ويأكلون لحماً طريّاً، فقلنا لهم: كُلُوا مِنْ طَيِّباتِ ما رَزَقْناكُمْ وَما ظَلَمُونا بمخالفتهم أمْرَ نبيهم وسوء أدبهم معه، حيث قالوا: فَاذْهَبْ أَنْتَ وَرَبُّكَ فَقاتِلا إِنَّا هاهُنا قاعِدُونَ، فعاقبهم بالتيه أربعين سنة، يتيهون في مقدار خمسة فراسخ أو ستة. وَلكِنْ ظلموا أنفسهم حيث أوقعوها في البلاء والمحنة.\nرُوِيَ أنهم لما أُمروا بجهاد الجبارين، جبنوا وقالوا تلك المقالة، فدعا عليهم سيدنا موسى عليه السلام فوقعوا في التيه بين مصر والشام، فكانوا يمشون النهار فيبيتون حيث أصبحوا، ويمشون الليل فيصبحون حيث أمسوا، فقالوا\nلموسى عليه السلام: من لنا بالطعام؟ فأنزل الله عليهم المنّ والسلوى، قالوا: كيف بحر الشمس؟ فظلل عليهم الغمام، قالوا:\nبم نستصبح بالليل؟ فضرب لهم عمود نور في وسط محلتهم، قالوا: من لنا بالماء؟ فأمر موسى عليه السلام بضرب الحجر، فقالوا: من لنا باللباس؟ فأعطوا ألا يَبْلى لهم ثوب، ولا يَخْلَق، ولا يَدْرن، وأن ينمو بنمو صاحبه، وقيل:\nكساهم مثل الظفر، وَاللَّهُ عَلى كُلِّ شَيْءٍ قَدِيرٌ.\nالإشارة: لما انفصلت الأرواح من عالم الجبروت، كانت على الطهارة الأصلية، والنزاهة الأزلية، عالمة بأسرار الربوبية وعظمة الألوهية، لكن لم يكن لها إلا جنة الحرية، دون جنة العبودية، فلما أراد الحق تعالى أن يمتعها بجنتين عن يمين وشمال، أمرها بالنزول إلى أرض العبودية، في ظلل من غمام البشرية، فمنَّ عليها بحلاوة المشاهدات وسلوان المناجاة، وقال لها: كلوا مِنْ طَيِّباتِ ما رَزَقْناكُمْ من طرائف العلوم، وفواكه الفهوم، هذا لمن اعتنى بروحه فاستكمل فضيلتها، وخالف هواها، فنفذت من عالم الأشباح إلى عالم الأرواح، فلم تنحجب بسحب الآثار إلى نفوذ شهود الأنوار، بل غابت عن شهود الآثار بشهود الأنوار. أما من حجبت عن شهود الأنوار بالوقوف مع الآثار، ووقعت في شبكة الحظوظ والشهوات، وربطت بعقال الأسباب والعادات، فقد ظلمت نفسها، وبخست حقها من مشاهدة مولاها، حتى اتسعت عليها دائرة الحس، ولم تنفذ إلى المشاهدة والأنس. وأنشدوا:\nكَمِّلْ حقيقتك التي لم تكمُلِ ... والجسمَ ضَعْهُ في الحضِيض الأسفلِ\nأَتُكَمِّلُ الفَانِي وَتَتْرُك باقياً ... هَمَلاً، وأَنت بأمرِه لم تحفلِ\nفالجسمُ للنفس النفيسةِ آلةٌ ... ما لَمْ تُحَصِّلْه بها لم يحْصُلِ\nيَفْنَى، وتَبقى دائماً في غِبْطةٍ ... أو شِقْوةٍ وندامة لا تنْجَلِي\nأعطيت جسمك خادما فخدمته ... أَتُمَلِّكُ المفضولَ رِقَّ الأفضلِ؟\nشَرَكٌ كثيفٌ أنتَ في أحباله ... مادام يُمكنك الخَلاصَ فَعَجِّلِ\nمَنْ يستطيعُ بلوغَ أَعْلَى مَنزلٍ ... ما بالَهُ يرضَى بأدنَى منزل!\nثم وبَّخهم على ما وقع منهم من المخالفة، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَظَلَّلْنا عَلَيْكُمُ الْغَمامَ وَأَنْزَلْنا عَلَيْكُمُ الْمَنَّ وَالسَّلْوى كُلُوا مِنْ طَيِّباتِ ما رَزَقْناكُمْ وَما ظَلَمُونا وَلكِنْ كانُوا أَنْفُسَهُمْ يَظْلِمُونَ (57)","quran_en":"57. We shaded you with clouds and sent down manna and quails for you: “Eat of the good things We have provided for you.” They did not wrong Us, but were wronging themselves.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Cloud, manna and quails","html":"<p>I say: <i>ghamām<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الغمام<\/bdi>) is thin cloud. Manna (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المنّ<\/bdi>) here is honey. <i>Salwā<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">السلوى<\/bdi>) is said to mean meat; more correctly, it is the name of a bird resembling the quail.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: ghamām ( الغمام ) is thin cloud. Manna ( المنّ ) here is honey. Salwā ( السلوى ) is said to mean meat; more correctly, it is the name of a bird resembling the quail."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Provision in the wandering","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, reminds the Children of Israel of His blessings during the wandering: We shaded you with clouds to protect you from the heat, and sent down manna, honey descending on the trees from dawn until sunrise, from which you scooped as much as you wished. We sent down <i>salwā<\/i>, birds gathered by the south wind and brought down upon you. You took as many as you wished, without their resisting, slaughtering them and eating fresh meat. We said: “Eat of the good things We have provided.” They did not wrong Us by disobeying their prophet and behaving discourteously towards him, saying, “Go, you and your Lord, and fight; we are sitting here” [5:24]. He punished them with forty years of wandering within five or six parasangs. They wronged themselves, bringing their own souls into affliction and trial.<\/p><p>It is related that, when commanded to fight the tyrants, they grew cowardly and made that reply. Our master Moses prayed against them and they fell into the wandering between Egypt and Syria. They walked by day but spent the night where they had begun the morning; they walked by night but reached morning where they had begun the evening. They asked<\/p><p>Moses, “Who will provide our food?” God sent manna and quails. “What of the sun’s heat?” He shaded them with clouds. “How shall we have light at night?” A pillar of light was set in the middle of their encampment. “Who will give us water?” Moses was commanded to strike the stone. “Who will provide clothing?” They were granted garments that neither wore out, grew old nor became dirty, and that grew with their wearers. Alternatively, they were clothed in a covering like a fingernail. God has power over all things.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, reminds the Children of Israel of His blessings during the wandering: We shaded you with clouds to protect you from the heat, and sent down manna, honey descending on the trees from dawn until sunrise, from which you scooped as much as you wished. We sent down salwā , birds gathered by the south wind and brought down upon you. You took as many as you wished, without their resisting, slaughtering them and eating fresh meat. We said: “Eat of the good things We have provided.” They did not wrong Us by disobeying their prophet and behaving discourteously towards him, saying, “Go, you and your Lord, and fight; we are sitting here” [5:24]. He punished them with forty years of wandering within five or six parasangs. They wronged themselves, bringing their own souls into affliction and trial. It is related that, when commanded to fight the tyrants, they grew cowardly and made that reply. Our master Moses prayed against them and they fell into the wandering between Egypt and Syria. They walked by day but spent the night where they had begun the morning; they walked by night but reached morning where they had begun the evening. They asked Moses, “Who will provide our food?” God sent manna and quails. “What of the sun’s heat?” He shaded them with clouds. “How shall we have light at night?” A pillar of light was set in the middle of their encampment. “Who will give us water?” Moses was commanded to strike the stone. “Who will provide clothing?” They were granted garments that neither wore out, grew old nor became dirty, and that grew with their wearers. Alternatively, they were clothed in a covering like a fingernail. God has power over all things."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Freedom, servanthood and the body’s rightful place","html":"<p>When the spirits separated from the world of the jabarūt, they possessed original purity and primordial freedom from defilement, knowing the mysteries of lordship and the greatness of divinity. They had only the garden of freedom, however, not the garden of servanthood. When the Real Most High wished them to enjoy two gardens, to right and left, He commanded them to descend to the earth of servanthood beneath the clouds of human embodiment. He favoured them with the sweetness of witnessing and the solace of intimate converse, telling them: eat of the good things We have provided—the rare treasures of sciences and fruits of understanding.<\/p><p>This applies to one who cares for his spirit, perfects its excellence, and opposes its caprice, so that it penetrates from the world of bodily forms to the world of spirits. The clouds of created effects do not prevent it from penetrating to witness the lights; rather, in witnessing the lights it is absent from witnessing effects. [Translator’s note: Cairo I:110 prints فلم تنحجب بسحب الآثار إلى نفوذ شهود الأنوار. The connection made by إلى (“to”) within this negative clause is unclear. The following contrast establishes the intended opposition between witnessing lights and being veiled by effects. Ibn ʿAjība’s <i>Īqāẓ al-himam<\/i> (ed. al-Kayyālī, 1437/2016, p. 230, ḥikma 110) likewise describes the clouds of effects clearing so that light shines and the heart is absent from forms. This supports the contextual English, but does not explain the exact preposition or establish an emendation.] A spirit veiled from the lights by stopping at effects, caught in the net of egoic shares and desires, and tethered by secondary causes and habits has wronged itself and diminished its rightful share of witnessing its Master. The sphere of sense perception expands over it, and it does not penetrate to witnessing and intimacy. The following verses are recited:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">Perfect your reality, still imperfect,<br>and put the body in the lowest place.<br>Will you perfect what passes away and leave what endures<br>neglected, taking no heed of its concern?<br>The body is an instrument for the precious soul:<br>what you do not acquire through it is not acquired.<br>It perishes, while you remain for ever<br>in joy, or misery and unrelenting regret.<br>You were given your body as a servant, yet served it:<br>will you give the inferior mastery over the superior?<br>It is a thick net, and you are in its cords:<br>while escape is possible, hasten!<br>One capable of reaching the highest dwelling—<br>why is he content with the lowest?<\/blockquote>","authorial":true,"text":"When the spirits separated from the world of the jabarūt, they possessed original purity and primordial freedom from defilement, knowing the mysteries of lordship and the greatness of divinity. They had only the garden of freedom, however, not the garden of servanthood. When the Real Most High wished them to enjoy two gardens, to right and left, He commanded them to descend to the earth of servanthood beneath the clouds of human embodiment. He favoured them with the sweetness of witnessing and the solace of intimate converse, telling them: eat of the good things We have provided—the rare treasures of sciences and fruits of understanding. This applies to one who cares for his spirit, perfects its excellence, and opposes its caprice, so that it penetrates from the world of bodily forms to the world of spirits. The clouds of created effects do not prevent it from penetrating to witness the lights; rather, in witnessing the lights it is absent from witnessing effects. [Translator’s note: Cairo I:110 prints فلم تنحجب بسحب الآثار إلى نفوذ شهود الأنوار. The connection made by إلى (“to”) within this negative clause is unclear. The following contrast establishes the intended opposition between witnessing lights and being veiled by effects. Ibn ʿAjība’s Īqāẓ al-himam (ed. al-Kayyālī, 1437/2016, p. 230, ḥikma 110) likewise describes the clouds of effects clearing so that light shines and the heart is absent from forms. This supports the contextual English, but does not explain the exact preposition or establish an emendation.] A spirit veiled from the lights by stopping at effects, caught in the net of egoic shares and desires, and tethered by secondary causes and habits has wronged itself and diminished its rightful share of witnessing its Master. The sphere of sense perception expands over it, and it does not penetrate to witnessing and intimacy. The following verses are recited: Perfect your reality, still imperfect, and put the body in the lowest place. Will you perfect what passes away and leave what endures neglected, taking no heed of its concern? The body is an instrument for the precious soul: what you do not acquire through it is not acquired. It perishes, while you remain for ever in joy, or misery and unrelenting regret. You were given your body as a servant, yet served it: will you give the inferior mastery over the superior? It is a thick net, and you are in its cords: while escape is possible, hasten! One capable of reaching the highest dwelling— why is he content with the lowest?"},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then rebukes their disobedience, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then rebukes their disobedience, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":4443,"arabic_characters":2858},{"id":"q2-58-59","q":"2:58–59","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:58–59 · Entering through humility","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 110–112","cairo":"I:110–112","source_page_first":110,"source_page_last":112,"ar":"وَإِذْ قُلْنَا ادْخُلُوا هذِهِ الْقَرْيَةَ فَكُلُوا مِنْها حَيْثُ شِئْتُمْ رَغَداً وَادْخُلُوا الْبابَ سُجَّداً وَقُولُوا حِطَّةٌ نَغْفِرْ لَكُمْ خَطاياكُمْ وَسَنَزِيدُ الْمُحْسِنِينَ (58) فَبَدَّلَ الَّذِينَ ظَلَمُوا قَوْلاً غَيْرَ الَّذِي قِيلَ لَهُمْ فَأَنْزَلْنا عَلَى الَّذِينَ ظَلَمُوا رِجْزاً مِنَ السَّماءِ بِما كانُوا يَفْسُقُونَ (59)\nقلت: (حِطة) : خبر مبتدأ مضمر، أي: أمرنا حطة، أي: تواضع وانحطاط، وقال هنا: (فكلوا) ، وفي الأعراف بالواو لأن الأكل مرتب على الدخول، بخلاف السكنى، فإنها تفارق الأكل، فكأنه مأمور به.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: واذكروا يا بني إسرائيل حين قلنا لأسلافكم بعد أن خرجوا من التيه: ادْخُلُوا هذِهِ الْقَرْيَةَ أعني بيت المقدس، أو أريحا، بعد أن تُجاهدوا أهلها، فَكُلُوا من نعم ما فيها أكلاً واسعاً لأنها مخصبة، وَادْخُلُوا باب القرية راكعين، تواضعاً وشكراً، وَقُولُوا في دخولكم: شأننا حِطَّةٌ، أي: شأننا الانحطاط والتواضع لله، فإن فعلتم ذلك نَغْفِرْ لَكُمْ خَطاياكُمْ، وَسَنَزِيدُ من امتثل أمرنا، وأحسن الأدب معنا، خيراً كثيراً، في الدنيا والآخرة، فَبَدَّلَ الَّذِينَ ظَلَمُوا منهم قولا غيْر الَّذِي أمروا به، وقالوا مكان حطة: حنطة، حبة في شعرة، فَأَنْزَلْنا عَلَى الَّذِينَ ظَلَمُوا رِجْزاً عذاباً مِنَ السَّماءِ قيل: هو الطاعون، فمات منهم سبعون ألفاً في يوم واحد، بسبب فسقهم وتعديهم الحدود.\nالإشارة: يقول الحق سبحانه للأرواح، لما كمل تطهيرها من البقايا، وتكاملت فيها المزايا: ادخلوا هذه الحضرة المقدسة، وتنعموا فيها حيث شئتم بالمشاهدة، والمكالمة، والمواجهة، والمساورة، والمفاتحة، والمناجاة، وادخلوا بابها أذلاء صاغرين، فلا دخول للحضرة المقدسة إلا من باب الذل والافتقار، وأنشدوا:\nومَا رُمتُ الدخولَ عليهِ حتَّى ... حَلَلْتُ مَحَلَّةَ العبدِ الذليلِ\nوأَغمضْتُ الجفُونَ على قَذَاها ... وصُنْت النَّفْسَ عن قالٍ وقيلِ(1)\nوقيل لأبي يزيد: يا أبا يزيد، خزائننا معمورة بالخدمة، ائتني من كُوّة الذل والافتقار. وفي رواية قيل له: يا أبا يزيد: تقرب إلينا بما ليس عندنا، فقال: يا رب وما الذي ليس عندك؟ فقال: الذل والافتقار. هـ. وقال شيخ المشايخ القطب الجيلاني رضى الله عنه (أتيت الأبواب كلها، فوجدت عليها الزحام، فأتيت من باب الذل والافتقار، فوجدته خالياً، فدخلت منه، وقلت: هلموا) . أو كما قال. وقال الشاعر:\nتذلَّلْ لمَن تَهْوَى فَلَيْسَ الْهَوى سَهْلُ(2) ... إِذَا رَضِيَ المحبوبُ صَحّ لكَ الوّصْلُ\nوقولوا عند دخولكم الحضرة: شأننا حطة أي: شأننا السفليات دون العلويات، فالسلوك من باب السفليات واجب، وإلا فلا وصول، فكل مَن سلك من باب السفليات طهر من البقايا، وتكاملت فيه المزايا، فيصلح لدخول الحضرة،\nوينخرط في سلك أهل الشهود والنظرة، فيكون من المحسنين المقربين، فلا جرم أن الله يزيده ترقياً في العلوم والأسرار، في هذه الدار، وفي تلك الدار، بخلاف مَن خالف ما أُمر به من سلوك طريق السفليات، وتعاطي الأمور العلويات، قبل كمال التربية فإنه يرجع إلى غم الحجاب، وسوء الحساب بسبب خروجه عن طريق الأحباب، وسلوكه طريق أهل الغفلة والارتياب. وبالله التوفيق، وهو الهادي إلى سواء الطريق.\nثم ذكَّرهم بنعمة الماء الذي سقاهم فى التيه، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَإِذْ قُلْنَا ادْخُلُوا هذِهِ الْقَرْيَةَ فَكُلُوا مِنْها حَيْثُ شِئْتُمْ رَغَداً وَادْخُلُوا الْبابَ سُجَّداً وَقُولُوا حِطَّةٌ نَغْفِرْ لَكُمْ خَطاياكُمْ وَسَنَزِيدُ الْمُحْسِنِينَ (58) فَبَدَّلَ الَّذِينَ ظَلَمُوا قَوْلاً غَيْرَ الَّذِي قِيلَ لَهُمْ فَأَنْزَلْنا عَلَى الَّذِينَ ظَلَمُوا رِجْزاً مِنَ السَّماءِ بِما كانُوا يَفْسُقُونَ (59)","quran_en":"58. When We said, “Enter this town and eat freely from it wherever you wish. Enter the gate bowing, and say, ‘Remission!’ We shall forgive your offences and increase the reward of those who do good.” 59. But the wrongdoers substituted a saying other than what they had been told. We therefore sent down upon the wrongdoers a scourge from heaven because they transgressed.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Ḥiṭṭa and the relation between entering and eating","html":"<p>I say: <i>ḥiṭṭa<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حطة<\/bdi>) is the predicate of an implied subject: “Our affair is abasement,” that is, humility and lowering ourselves. Here He says “then eat” with <i>fā<\/i>, whereas in al-Aʿrāf He uses <i>wāw<\/i>: eating follows upon entering, unlike dwelling, which can occur apart from eating, so eating is as though independently commanded.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-49\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B045]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: ḥiṭṭa ( حطة ) is the predicate of an implied subject: “Our affair is abasement,” that is, humility and lowering ourselves. Here He says “then eat” with fā , whereas in al-Aʿrāf He uses wāw : eating follows upon entering, unlike dwelling, which can occur apart from eating, so eating is as though independently commanded. [B045]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Courtesy in receiving a blessing","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: Children of Israel, remember when, after your forebears left the wandering, We told them: enter this town—Jerusalem or Jericho—after fighting its people, and eat abundantly of its blessings, for it is fertile. Enter its gate bowing in humility and gratitude. On entering, say, “Our affair is <i>ḥiṭṭa<\/i>”: our condition is to lower and humble ourselves before God. If you do so, We forgive your offences and add much good in this world and the Hereafter for those who obey Our command and show good courtesy towards Us.<\/p><p>The wrongdoers among them substituted another saying: instead of <i>ḥiṭṭa<\/i>, they said <i>ḥinṭa<\/i>, “wheat,” and “a grain in a hair.” We sent down upon the wrongdoers a scourge, a punishment from heaven, said to have been plague. Seventy thousand died in a single day because of their transgression and exceeding the limits.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: Children of Israel, remember when, after your forebears left the wandering, We told them: enter this town—Jerusalem or Jericho—after fighting its people, and eat abundantly of its blessings, for it is fertile. Enter its gate bowing in humility and gratitude. On entering, say, “Our affair is ḥiṭṭa ”: our condition is to lower and humble ourselves before God. If you do so, We forgive your offences and add much good in this world and the Hereafter for those who obey Our command and show good courtesy towards Us. The wrongdoers among them substituted another saying: instead of ḥiṭṭa , they said ḥinṭa , “wheat,” and “a grain in a hair.” We sent down upon the wrongdoers a scourge, a punishment from heaven, said to have been plague. Seventy thousand died in a single day because of their transgression and exceeding the limits."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The unfrequented door of need","html":"<p>Once spirits are fully purified of residual selfhood and their excellences complete, the Real—glory be to Him—says: enter this holy Presence and delight as you wish in witnessing, discourse, face-to-face encounter, <i>musāwara<\/i> [a figurative encounter whose precise force remains uncertain], opening address and secret converse [Translator’s note: Cairo I:111 reads <i>al-musāwara<\/i>. Ibn ʿAjība also includes the word in <i>Īqāẓ al-himam<\/i> (ed. al-Kayyālī, 1437/2016, p. 384, ḥikma 203) among figurative expressions for what spirits perceive and inward selves taste of the Real’s greatness and majesty. This confirms the term, but does not define it individually. Ibn Manẓūr’s <i>Lisān al-ʿArab<\/i>, s.v. سور, glosses <i>sāwarahu musāwaratan<\/i> as assailing or springing at another. Neither “intimate drawing near” nor a more forceful mystical equivalent is established as Ibn ʿAjība’s precise meaning here. The technical word is therefore retained; no emendation to <i>musārara<\/i> or <i>musāmara<\/i> is warranted.] (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المشاهدة والمكالمة والمواجهة والمساورة والمفاتحة والمناجاة<\/bdi>). Enter its gate abased and humble: there is no entrance to the holy Presence except through humility and need. The following is recited:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">I did not seek entry unto Him until<br>I took the station of the lowly servant,<br>closed my eyelids over their discomfort,<br>and guarded the soul from gossip and disputation.<\/blockquote><p>Abū Yazīd was told: “Our treasuries are full of service: come to Me through the aperture of humility and need.” Another account says: “Abū Yazīd, draw near to Us with what We do not possess.” He asked, “Lord, what do You not possess?” He replied: “Humility and need.” End of quotation.<\/p><p>The shaykh of shaykhs, the Pole al-Jīlānī, God be pleased with him, said: “I approached every door and found crowds. I came to the door of humility and need and found it empty; I entered through it and called, ‘Come!’”—or words to that effect. The poet says:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">Humble yourself before the one you love: love is not easy.<br>When the beloved is pleased, union is assured.<\/blockquote><p>On entering the Presence say, “Our affair is <i>ḥiṭṭa<\/i>”: our concern is lowliness, not loftiness. Travelling through the door of lowly conditions is necessary; otherwise there is no arrival. Whoever travels by that door is purified of residues and perfected in excellences, becoming fit for entry into the Presence<\/p><p>and inclusion among the people of witnessing and vision. He belongs to the excellent, those brought near; necessarily God increases his ascent in sciences and mysteries, in this abode and the next. By contrast, one who disobeys the command to follow lowly conditions and assumes lofty concerns before his formation is complete returns to the gloom of the veil and an evil reckoning, because he has left the beloveds’ road for the path of heedlessness and doubt. Through God is success, and He guides to the even way.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Once spirits are fully purified of residual selfhood and their excellences complete, the Real—glory be to Him—says: enter this holy Presence and delight as you wish in witnessing, discourse, face-to-face encounter, musāwara [a figurative encounter whose precise force remains uncertain], opening address and secret converse [Translator’s note: Cairo I:111 reads al-musāwara . Ibn ʿAjība also includes the word in Īqāẓ al-himam (ed. al-Kayyālī, 1437/2016, p. 384, ḥikma 203) among figurative expressions for what spirits perceive and inward selves taste of the Real’s greatness and majesty. This confirms the term, but does not define it individually. Ibn Manẓūr’s Lisān al-ʿArab , s.v. سور, glosses sāwarahu musāwaratan as assailing or springing at another. Neither “intimate drawing near” nor a more forceful mystical equivalent is established as Ibn ʿAjība’s precise meaning here. The technical word is therefore retained; no emendation to musārara or musāmara is warranted.] ( المشاهدة والمكالمة والمواجهة والمساورة والمفاتحة والمناجاة ). Enter its gate abased and humble: there is no entrance to the holy Presence except through humility and need. The following is recited: I did not seek entry unto Him until I took the station of the lowly servant, closed my eyelids over their discomfort, and guarded the soul from gossip and disputation. Abū Yazīd was told: “Our treasuries are full of service: come to Me through the aperture of humility and need.” Another account says: “Abū Yazīd, draw near to Us with what We do not possess.” He asked, “Lord, what do You not possess?” He replied: “Humility and need.” End of quotation. The shaykh of shaykhs, the Pole al-Jīlānī, God be pleased with him, said: “I approached every door and found crowds. I came to the door of humility and need and found it empty; I entered through it and called, ‘Come!’”—or words to that effect. The poet says: Humble yourself before the one you love: love is not easy. When the beloved is pleased, union is assured. On entering the Presence say, “Our affair is ḥiṭṭa ”: our concern is lowliness, not loftiness. Travelling through the door of lowly conditions is necessary; otherwise there is no arrival. Whoever travels by that door is purified of residues and perfected in excellences, becoming fit for entry into the Presence and inclusion among the people of witnessing and vision. He belongs to the excellent, those brought near; necessarily God increases his ascent in sciences and mysteries, in this abode and the next. By contrast, one who disobeys the command to follow lowly conditions and assumes lofty concerns before his formation is complete returns to the gloom of the veil and an evil reckoning, because he has left the beloveds’ road for the path of heedlessness and doubt. Through God is success, and He guides to the even way."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He reminds them of the blessing of the water He gave them during the wandering, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He reminds them of the blessing of the water He gave them during the wandering, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C111.1","page":111,"ar":"الأبيات للسري السقطي، كما في زاد المسير لابن الجوزي.","en":"The verses are by al-Sarī al-Saqaṭī, as stated in Ibn al-Jawzī’s Zād al-masīr.","anchor":"وما رمت الدخول عليه حتى حللت محلة العبد الذليل …"},{"id":"C111.2","page":111,"ar":"راجع التعليق على هذا الشطر ص ١٠٢.","en":"See the note on this hemistich on page 102.","anchor":"تذلل لمن تهوى فليس الهوى سهل"}],"english_characters":4117,"arabic_characters":2735},{"id":"q2-60","q":"2:60","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:60 · The twelve springs and the diversity of spiritual gifts","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 112–113","cairo":"I:112–113","source_page_first":112,"source_page_last":113,"ar":"وَإِذِ اسْتَسْقى مُوسى لِقَوْمِهِ فَقُلْنَا اضْرِبْ بِعَصاكَ الْحَجَرَ فَانْفَجَرَتْ مِنْهُ اثْنَتا عَشْرَةَ عَيْناً قَدْ عَلِمَ كُلُّ أُناسٍ مَشْرَبَهُمْ كُلُوا وَاشْرَبُوا مِنْ رِزْقِ اللَّهِ وَلا تَعْثَوْا فِي الْأَرْضِ مُفْسِدِينَ (60)\nقلت: اسْتَسْقى: طلب السقي، و «ال» في الْحَجَرَ للعهد، وهو الحجر الذي فرَّ بثوبه، أو حجر خفيف مربع مثل رأس الرجل، أُمر أن يحمله معه، فكان يضعه في مخلاته، فإذا احتاج الماء ضربه، قيل: كان من رخام، وقيل: كان كذَّان(1)، كان فيه اثنتا عشرة حفرة، تنبع من كل حفرة عين ماء عذب، على عدد الأسباط، فإذا أراد حمله ضربه فجفّ الماء منه، وقيل: للجنس، فكان يضرب أيَّ حجر وجد، فتنفجر منه عيوناً، ثم تسير كل عين في جدول إلى سبط، فقالوا: إن أفضينا إلى أرض لا حجارة فيها عطشنا، فأوحى إليه: أن كلِّمْهُ يَطِعْك لعلهم يعتبرون.\nوفَانْفَجَرَتْ: معطوف على محذوف أي: فضرب فانفجرت، والعُثو: أشد الفساد، عثا يعثو عثواً، وعثى يعثِي عثِياً، وعاث يعيث عيثا، ومُفْسِدِينَ: حال مؤكدة لعاملها، أو مقيدة، إن قلنا: إن العثو أعم من الفساد، لصدقه على القصاص، فإنه عثو غير فساد. انظر البيضاوي.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: واذكروا يا بني إسرائيل حين عطشتم في التيه، فطلبتم من موسى السقي، فاستسقى لكم، فَقُلْنَا له: اضْرِبْ بِعَصاكَ التي أخذتها من شعيب عليه السلام، وكانت من آس الجنة، وورثت عن آدم عليه السلام، فيها عشرة أذرع، فضرب فَانْفَجَرَتْ مِنْهُ اثْنَتا عَشْرَةَ عَيْناً على عدد أسباطكم، فكل عين تجري إلى سبط قَدْ عَلِمَ كُلُّ أُناسٍ مَشْرَبَهُمْ مُعَيناً، لا يعدو أحد على أحد، فقلنا لهم: كُلُوا من المن والسلوى، وَاشْرَبُوا من الماء الذي رزقناكم، ولا تطغوا بالنعم فتفسدوا في الأرض بالمعاصي والذنوب، فيكون ذلك كفراً مستوجباً للسلب بعد العطاء، رُوِيَ أنهم كانوا ستمائة ألف، وسعة المعسكر اثنا عشر ميلاً. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: اعلم أن الأرواح إذا تطهرت من الأكدار، وتحررت من الأغيار، وأشرقت عليها الأنوار والأسرار، وكمل تطهيرها، وتمت تصفيتها، كان صاحبها آية من آيات الله، وحجة من حجج الله، إذا ضرب بعصا همته القلوب القاسية أو الأنفس الأبية، لانت وانفجرت بالعلوم القدسية، كل واحد بما يليق به، فمنها من تنبع بالعلوم الوهبية، ومنها من تنبع بالعلوم الرسمية، ومنها من تنبع بالكرامات وخوارق العادات، ومنها من تنبع منها المكاشفات والاطلاعات، قد علم كل أناس مشربهم، على حسب ما سبق لهم، فيقول الحق تعالى لهم: كلوا من ثمرات ما اجتنيتم من العلوم والمعارف التي أوليناكم، واشربوا من مناهل المنازل التي فيها أقمناكم، أو كلوا من ثمرات المعرفة ما تتقوى به معانيكم، واشربوا من خمر الحبيب ما تغيبوا به عن وجودكم، ولا تتعدوا أطواركم من القيام بوظائف العبودية، ومعرفة عظمة الربوبية، فتكونوا لسلب ما أولاكم متعرضين، ولعقوبته مستحقين، عائذاً بالله من السلب بعد العطاء. آمين.\nولما سئموا من المن والسلوى، استبدلوا غيرهما، كما أشار إلى ذلك الحق تعالى، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَإِذِ اسْتَسْقى مُوسى لِقَوْمِهِ فَقُلْنَا اضْرِبْ بِعَصاكَ الْحَجَرَ فَانْفَجَرَتْ مِنْهُ اثْنَتا عَشْرَةَ عَيْناً قَدْ عَلِمَ كُلُّ أُناسٍ مَشْرَبَهُمْ كُلُوا وَاشْرَبُوا مِنْ رِزْقِ اللَّهِ وَلا تَعْثَوْا فِي الْأَرْضِ مُفْسِدِينَ (60)","quran_en":"60. When Moses sought water for his people, We said, “Strike the stone with your staff.” Twelve springs gushed from it, each people knowing its drinking-place. “Eat and drink of God’s provision, and do not spread corruption on earth.”","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The stone and the meaning of corruption","html":"<p>I say: seeking water (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الاستسقاء<\/bdi>) is requesting a supply of drink. The article in “the stone” specifies a known object: either the stone that fled with his garment, or a light, square stone, the size of a man’s head, which he was commanded to carry. He kept it in his bag and struck it when water was needed. It is said to have been marble, or soft porous stone (<i>kadhdhān<\/i>, <bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كذّان<\/bdi>). It contained twelve hollows, each producing a fresh spring, according to the number of tribes. When he wanted to carry it, he struck it and the water dried up. Alternatively, the article denotes the kind: he struck any stone he found, and springs burst out, each flowing in a channel to a tribe. They said, “If we reach a land without stones, we shall thirst.” He was inspired: “Speak to it and it will obey you, so that they may take heed.”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-50\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B046]<\/a><\/p><p>“Then they gushed forth” is coordinated with an omitted clause: “he struck, and they gushed forth.” <i>ʿUthūw<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العُثو<\/bdi>) is the most intense corruption. The verbal forms are <i>ʿathā–yaʿthū–ʿuthūwan<\/i>, <i>ʿathiya–yaʿthī–ʿithiyyan<\/i>, and <i>ʿātha–yaʿīthu–ʿaythan<\/i>. “Corrupting” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مفسدين<\/bdi>) is a circumstantial expression emphasizing its governing verb; alternatively, it restricts its meaning if <i>ʿuthūw<\/i> is taken more broadly than corruption, applying also to lawful retaliation, which is destructive action without corruption. See al-Bayḍāwī.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: seeking water ( الاستسقاء ) is requesting a supply of drink. The article in “the stone” specifies a known object: either the stone that fled with his garment, or a light, square stone, the size of a man’s head, which he was commanded to carry. He kept it in his bag and struck it when water was needed. It is said to have been marble, or soft porous stone ( kadhdhān , كذّان ). It contained twelve hollows, each producing a fresh spring, according to the number of tribes. When he wanted to carry it, he struck it and the water dried up. Alternatively, the article denotes the kind: he struck any stone he found, and springs burst out, each flowing in a channel to a tribe. They said, “If we reach a land without stones, we shall thirst.” He was inspired: “Speak to it and it will obey you, so that they may take heed.” [B046] “Then they gushed forth” is coordinated with an omitted clause: “he struck, and they gushed forth.” ʿUthūw ( العُثو ) is the most intense corruption. The verbal forms are ʿathā–yaʿthū–ʿuthūwan , ʿathiya–yaʿthī–ʿithiyyan , and ʿātha–yaʿīthu–ʿaythan . “Corrupting” ( مفسدين ) is a circumstantial expression emphasizing its governing verb; alternatively, it restricts its meaning if ʿuthūw is taken more broadly than corruption, applying also to lawful retaliation, which is destructive action without corruption. See al-Bayḍāwī."},{"type":"exposition","title":"A drinking-place for every tribe","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: Children of Israel, remember when you thirsted in the wandering and asked Moses for water. He sought it for you, and We told him: strike with the staff received from Shuʿayb, peace be upon him. It was of Paradise’s myrtle, inherited from Adam and ten cubits long. He struck, and twelve springs gushed forth, according to your tribes, each flowing to one tribe. Every group knew its appointed drinking-place, none encroaching upon another. We told them: eat the manna and quails and drink the water We have provided. Do not grow insolent through blessings and corrupt the earth with sins and disobedience: that is ingratitude deserving deprivation after bestowal. It is related that they numbered six hundred thousand and their camp extended twelve miles. And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: Children of Israel, remember when you thirsted in the wandering and asked Moses for water. He sought it for you, and We told him: strike with the staff received from Shuʿayb, peace be upon him. It was of Paradise’s myrtle, inherited from Adam and ten cubits long. He struck, and twelve springs gushed forth, according to your tribes, each flowing to one tribe. Every group knew its appointed drinking-place, none encroaching upon another. We told them: eat the manna and quails and drink the water We have provided. Do not grow insolent through blessings and corrupt the earth with sins and disobedience: that is ingratitude deserving deprivation after bestowal. It is related that they numbered six hundred thousand and their camp extended twelve miles. And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The shaykh’s aspiration and the disciples’ differing receptivities","html":"<p>Know that, when spirits are purified of impurities, freed from other-than-God, illuminated by lights and mysteries, and perfected in purification, their possessor becomes one of God’s signs and proofs. If he strikes hard hearts or resistant souls with the staff of his aspiration (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الهمة<\/bdi>), they soften and burst forth with holy sciences, each according to its capacity. Some yield bestowed sciences; others, formal disciplines; others, saintly wonders and extraordinary acts; others, unveilings and disclosures. Every people knows its drinking-place according to its prior allotment.<\/p><p>The Real Most High tells them: eat the fruits you have gathered of the sciences and forms of knowledge We bestowed, and drink from the watering-places of the stations in which We established you. Or: eat fruits of gnosis that strengthen your inward meanings, and drink the Beloved’s wine through which you become absent from your own existence. Do not exceed your bounds in fulfilling the duties of servanthood and recognizing the greatness of lordship, thereby exposing yourselves to the withdrawal of His gifts and deserving punishment. In God we take refuge from deprivation after bestowal. Amen.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Know that, when spirits are purified of impurities, freed from other-than-God, illuminated by lights and mysteries, and perfected in purification, their possessor becomes one of God’s signs and proofs. If he strikes hard hearts or resistant souls with the staff of his aspiration ( الهمة ), they soften and burst forth with holy sciences, each according to its capacity. Some yield bestowed sciences; others, formal disciplines; others, saintly wonders and extraordinary acts; others, unveilings and disclosures. Every people knows its drinking-place according to its prior allotment. The Real Most High tells them: eat the fruits you have gathered of the sciences and forms of knowledge We bestowed, and drink from the watering-places of the stations in which We established you. Or: eat fruits of gnosis that strengthen your inward meanings, and drink the Beloved’s wine through which you become absent from your own existence. Do not exceed your bounds in fulfilling the duties of servanthood and recognizing the greatness of lordship, thereby exposing yourselves to the withdrawal of His gifts and deserving punishment. In God we take refuge from deprivation after bestowal. Amen."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>When they tired of manna and quails, they sought something else instead, as the Real Most High indicates:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"When they tired of manna and quails, they sought something else instead, as the Real Most High indicates:"}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C112.1","page":112,"ar":"الكذان: جمع كذانة، وهي حجارة فيها رخاوة، وربما كانت نخرة. قلت: لا يبنى على تعيين هذا الحجر أمر ديني. والأسلم تفويض علمه إلى الله تعالى.","en":"Kadhdhān is the plural of kadhdhāna: stones with some softness, which may be hollow or worn within. I [the editor] say: no religious matter depends on identifying this stone. It is safer to entrust knowledge of it to God Most High.","anchor":"وقيل: كان كذان"}],"english_characters":3474,"arabic_characters":2532}];
